## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / completion/ending / future-commons-session-3
People can challenge and revise the community's shared rules. They still need to organize care, divide work fairly and make room for different ways of living.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / story body / future-commons-session-3
The next morning, we return to a cleared worktable. How can people affected by shared resources challenge and revise the rules? Care, fair work and disagreement still need attention.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-critical-edition
An editor compares manuscripts and shows uncertain readings. Readers can judge the choices instead of receiving an unexplained final version.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-critical-edition
An edition that shows its doubts

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-strict-ptolemy-challenged
Ptolemy's standard model keeps Venus on Earth's side of the Sun, so it cannot show nearly full phases. Observing those phases rules out that arrangement, but not every model in which Earth stays still.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-strict-ptolemy-challenged
Strict Ptolemaic Venus is challenged

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-age-still-open
Knowing which events came first does not tell us how many years separated them. Rock order and numerical age are different questions.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-age-still-open
Sequence is not duration

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-branching-descent
Similarities among living things can reflect shared ancestry. Their history branches rather than forming a ladder with humans as its required top.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-branching-descent
Resemblance can record shared descent

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-earth-age-measured
Earth formed about 4.54 billion years ago. The estimate draws on radioactive dating and evidence from the early solar system, not the order of rock layers alone.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-earth-age-measured
Earth is about 4.54 billion years old

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-industrial-lead-footprint
Lead appears even in laboratory tests without an ancient sample, revealing contamination that interferes with dating. Comparing older and modern environments turns that measurement problem into evidence of industrial lead pollution.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-industrial-lead-footprint
An analytical nuisance exposes industrial pollution

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / milestone-unfinished-common-world
People can challenge and revise the community's shared rules. They still disagree about how to divide work, provide care and prevent people from being forced into decisions.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / milestone-unfinished-common-world
A shared world with obligations still disputed

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-sky-archive
The archive records what people saw and marks the nights when they could not observe. Missing evidence is not treated as evidence that nothing happened.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-sky-archive
A sky archive with its gaps

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-eclipse-pattern
Eclipses recur in patterns. Those patterns help predict when to watch, but not every eclipse is visible from every place.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-eclipse-pattern
Recurring eclipse seasons

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-watch-window
The records suggest when an eclipse may occur. Plan a watch, but clouds or your location may prevent you from seeing one.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-watch-window
A watch, not a promised eclipse

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-portent-and-pattern
People can predict an eclipse and still give it a religious or political meaning. Prediction does not automatically replace those beliefs.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-portent-and-pattern
Prediction alongside portent

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-seasonal-calendar
A calendar connects the seasons with farming and food storage. It helps people prepare before the next flood or harvest.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-seasonal-calendar
A calendar for fields and stores

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-shared-year
Agreed dates let a community organize work and festivals together. People can share a calendar without agreeing about the nature of the universe.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-shared-year
A publicly shared year

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-natural-eclipse
Earth's shadow explains why the Moon darkens during a lunar eclipse. This connects a repeated observation with a physical cause.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-natural-eclipse
An eclipse has a natural cause

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-conditional-cause
An explanation should say when it applies. A lunar eclipse requires Earth to block the sunlight that would otherwise reach the Moon.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-conditional-cause
An explanation tied to conditions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-debt-objection
A proposed rule says justice means returning what you owe. But returning a weapon to someone who may harm others would cause harm. This example shows why repayment alone cannot define justice.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-debt-objection
Repayment can do harm

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-examined-definition
The rule that justice means repaying debts fails when returning a weapon could cause harm. Finding that problem gives a reason to keep questioning the definition; it does not yet supply a complete new one.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-examined-definition
A definition still under examination

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-examined-life
An examined life means questioning how you live and why. The aim is to judge your choices, not simply accept praise or custom.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-examined-life
The examined life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cause-and-meaning
Explaining how an eclipse happens does not settle what it means to a community. This comparison separates a physical cause from a religious or social interpretation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cause-and-meaning
Cause need not erase meaning

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-answerable-claims
A claim should be open to questions and difficult examples. The standing of the person making it is not enough to settle whether it is right.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-answerable-claims
Claims answerable to objections

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-stable-forms
Plato proposes unchanging Forms, such as Beauty itself, to explain what changing examples share. The beautiful things we encounter are imperfect and changeable, unlike the Form he describes.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-stable-forms
Plato: stable Forms

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-form-in-particular
Aristotle places form in the particular thing: what makes this living thing the kind of thing it is. This differs from Plato's separate Forms.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-form-in-particular
Aristotle: form in the particular

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-animal-kinds
Comparing animals' parts and activities gives a basis for grouping them. One shared feature alone may not be enough to establish a group.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-animal-kinds
Animals compared by structures and activities

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-part-and-function
Aristotle understands an animal's parts through the organization of the whole animal. An organ is not explained only by listing its material: its role in that living animal also matters.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-part-and-function
An organ understood through the whole animal

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-four-causes
Aristotle distinguishes four kinds of explanation: what something is made of, its form, what produces it, and its purpose. One answer need not replace the others.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-four-causes
Different questions about why

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-valid-inference
A valid argument has a conclusion that must follow if its starting claims are true. It still matters whether those claims are actually true.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-valid-inference
A conclusion follows from premises

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-valid-not-true
An argument can follow the rules of logic and still start from a false claim. Check both the reasoning and its starting claims.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-valid-not-true
Validity is not truth

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cultivated-virtue
For Aristotle, living well requires habits and practical judgment developed through action. Knowing a rule is not the same as being able to act well.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cultivated-virtue
Virtue as cultivated judgment

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-turning-attention
In Plato's cave story, a prisoner learns that familiar shadows are not the whole world. Education means reconsidering what he takes for reality, not simply receiving more facts about the shadows.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-turning-attention
Education as a turning of attention

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cynic-sufficiency
Stories about Diogenes contrast his few wants with a ruler's offer of wealth. Needing less can make it harder for a powerful patron to buy someone's loyalty. This ideal does not mean that forced poverty is harmless.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cynic-sufficiency
Freedom by needing less

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-convention-questioned
Cities disagree about what counts as respectable. A custom's local popularity therefore cannot, by itself, settle whether it is a good way to live.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-convention-questioned
Local respectability does not settle a good life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-stoic-control
Stoic practice focuses effort on judgments and responses rather than things we cannot control. It does not deny the pain of loss or injustice.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-stoic-control
Care for the judgment that is yours

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cosmopolis
Stoic thinkers connect care for one's own judgments with concern for other people who share in reason. Their idea of fellowship can extend beyond the boundaries of a single city.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cosmopolis
Fellowship beyond one city

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-release-from-terror
Epicurus argues that there is no suffering after death because there is no remaining sensation. The argument offers relief from fear without denying grief or ending disagreement.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-release-from-terror
Freedom from the terror of death

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-garden-pleasure
Epicurus places friendship, security and simple enjoyment at the centre of a good life. Seeking pleasure in this account does not mean endlessly increasing wealth or indulgence.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-garden-pleasure
Pleasure in friendship and sufficiency

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-suspended-assent
When the available accounts conflict and neither settles the question, a skeptic can withhold agreement. Leaving judgment open is different from proving that both sides are false or that knowledge is impossible.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-suspended-assent
A judgment left suspended

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-deductive-geometry
A geometric proof connects a conclusion to stated starting points through logical steps. It explains why the conclusion follows.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-deductive-geometry
A connected geometric proof

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-spherical-earth
A spherical Earth helps explain why observers in different places see different stars and shadows. It joins local observations into one model.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-spherical-earth
A spherical Earth

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-measurable-earth
Different stars and shadows at different places support a curved Earth. Adding the measured distance between observing places makes it possible to investigate the size of that whole Earth.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-measurable-earth
An Earth whose size can be investigated

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-planetary-archive
Records collected over generations let astronomers compare planetary movements. Greek astronomy also drew on earlier Babylonian observations.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-planetary-archive
An archive of planetary motion

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-ptolemaic-model
Ptolemy's Earth-centered model predicts celestial positions using combined circular motions. Its historical value includes what it could predict, not just what later accounts changed.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-ptolemaic-model
Ptolemy’s predictive heavens

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-model-not-cause
A model of planetary motion may accurately predict where a planet appears. That success does not, by itself, establish every physical cause or assumption in the model.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-model-not-cause
A forecast is not its physical cause

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mechanical-sky
Gears can represent repeating movements in the sky. A physical model makes those cycles visible without explaining every cause of them.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mechanical-sky
A sky represented in gears

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-textual-variants
Different versions of a text are kept visible for comparison. Their differences become evidence about how the work was copied and changed.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-textual-variants
A text with visible alternatives

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-arabic-logic
Translators carry logical arguments into Arabic while developing terms for their distinctions. The texts become available for new teaching and debate.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-arabic-logic
Logic in a new scholarly language

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-arabic-nature
Arabic-speaking scholars use, criticize and develop inherited accounts of nature. Translation supports new inquiry rather than simply storing old knowledge.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-arabic-nature
Natural philosophy in Arabic

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-ptolemy-questioned
Arabic-speaking astronomers use new observations to criticize difficulties in Ptolemy's inherited sky model. Learning from an older work can lead to revision rather than unquestioning acceptance.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-ptolemy-questioned
Ptolemy read critically

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-positional-reckoning
Place-value notation gives a practical way to record and work with quantities. This mathematical tradition did not come from a single Greek source.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-positional-reckoning
Place-value reckoning

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-algebraic-relations
Relations between known and unknown quantities can be studied before the unknown value is found. This makes a problem easier to state and reason about.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-algebraic-relations
Reasoning about an unknown share

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-contingent-being
Avicenna distinguishes what a thing is from whether it exists. A thing that could exist or fail to exist still needs an explanation for why it exists at all.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-contingent-being
Avicenna: contingent existence

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-necessary-being
Avicenna argues that things whose existence depends on something else require a source that does not depend on another. His proposed necessary being answers why dependent things exist at all.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-necessary-being
Avicenna: a necessary source of existence

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-active-commentary
A commentary becomes new inquiry when it tests and develops the older argument. Receiving a tradition can also change it.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-active-commentary
A commentary that changes the question

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-vision-dispute
Some theories say sight works through rays leaving the eye, while another account studies light entering it. Comparing the claims turns familiar seeing into a question that observations can test.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-vision-dispute
Rival accounts of sight

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-light-into-eye
Ibn al-Haytham explains sight through light entering the eye. This directs inquiry toward light sources, paths and viewing conditions.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-light-into-eye
Sight depends on light entering the eye

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-geometric-optics
Drawing light paths helps explain images and sight. Geometry connects the arrangement of objects with where their light arrives.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-geometric-optics
Light paths as a geometric account

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-inverted-image
Light passing through the small opening forms an upside-down image on the screen. The dark box makes the faint image easier to see.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-inverted-image
An inverted image on the screen

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-ground-lens
The glass has been shaped into a convex lens. Held at a suitable distance, it can make a nearby object appear larger.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-ground-lens
A convex magnifying lens

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-magnified-view
The lens bends light so that a nearby object appears larger. The view depends on how the lens and object are positioned.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-magnified-view
A magnified close-up view

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-seeing-and-judging
Light entering the eye does not explain every judgment we make about what we see. Distance, size and other features can still be misread.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-seeing-and-judging
Seeing includes interpretation

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-latin-aristotle
Latin translations make Aristotle's works available in new schools and debates. Readers also encounter different interpretations of those works.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-latin-aristotle
Aristotle in Latin

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-commentaries-in-dispute
Commentators disagree about what an older argument means and whether it succeeds. There is no single uncontested reading.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-commentaries-in-dispute
The commentators disagree

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-reason-faith-distinction
Aquinas distinguishes truths investigated by natural reason from truths accepted through revelation. The distinction does not make the two inquiries identical.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-reason-faith-distinction
Reason and faith: distinct questions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-formal-disputation
An objection receives a reasoned reply in an organized debate. The format makes disagreement explicit; it does not guarantee agreement.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-formal-disputation
An objection and an accountable reply

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-real-universal
A realist account says that separate things share a real common nature. Medieval versions differ from Plato's theory of separately existing Forms.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-real-universal
A shared nature beyond this instance

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-nominal-kinds
A nominalist account uses common names and concepts for separate things without positing a shared universal thing outside the mind. This is more than rejecting Plato's separate Forms.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-nominal-kinds
Common concepts, not a shared thing outside the mind

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-universals-open
A realist account says separate things share a real common nature. A nominalist account groups individuals through names and concepts without such a shared thing outside the mind.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-universals-open
One shared name, rival accounts

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-explanatory-economy
Do not add things to an explanation when they do no work. A shorter explanation is not automatically true; it must still account for the evidence.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-explanatory-economy
Do not multiply idle explanations

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-intelligible-nature
For Aquinas, studying natural causes can help people understand a created world. Religious belief need not make every event an unexplained miracle.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-intelligible-nature
Aquinas: nature has its own intelligibility

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-reproducible-page
Printing makes repeated copies of a prepared page possible. Different cultures developed different ways to use that possibility.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-reproducible-page
A reproducible printed page

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-type-printed-edition
Reusable type allows an edition to be printed in many copies. It expands circulation without removing the need for editing.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-type-printed-edition
An edition made with reusable type

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-compared-edition
Printed copies let more readers compare the same text. The editor's uncertain choices still need to be visible.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-compared-edition
Printed copies with inspectable variants

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-parallel-translations
Greek and Latin versions can be read side by side. Comparing them helps readers question earlier translation choices.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-parallel-translations
Greek and Latin versions side by side

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-translation-choices
A translation involves choices about meaning. Showing those choices lets readers see where another interpretation may be possible.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-translation-choices
A translation’s choices made visible

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-ancient-wisdom-claim
Some Renaissance readers connected Plato with writings attributed to Hermes because they believed both offered ancient wisdom. The claimed great age of the Hermetic texts helped give them authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-ancient-wisdom-claim
A claim to a primordial wisdom

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-redated-hermetica
Renaissance readers had treated the writings attributed to Hermes as extremely ancient. Evidence from their language places them later. This weakens the appeal to antiquity without automatically refuting every idea they contain.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-redated-hermetica
Hermetic wisdom, later than claimed

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-contested-publication
Printing brings knowledge to more readers while also creating new struggles over censorship and authority. Wider circulation is not the same as unrestricted speech.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-contested-publication
Printed knowledge under contention

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-printed-euclid
A printed geometric proof can reach many readers in comparable copies. They can examine the same reasoning and challenge its steps.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-printed-euclid
A geometric proof that many readers can compare

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-viewpoint-occlusion
A fixed viewpoint hides some surfaces behind others. What a picture leaves out can change when the viewer moves.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-viewpoint-occlusion
A fixed view leaves other surfaces hidden

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mapped-globe
A shared system of positions connects individual places across Earth. It lets observations from different places be compared.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mapped-globe
An Earth with comparable positions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-projection-tradeoff
Flattening Earth involves choices. This chart makes compass directions useful while greatly enlarging areas near the poles.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-projection-tradeoff
Useful bearings, distorted areas

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-telescope
The telescope makes distant objects appear larger. It opens new observations, but interpreting them still requires argument and comparison.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-telescope
A telescope

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-rough-moon
The Moon's shadows suggest a rough surface. This challenges a strict contrast between perfect heavens and an imperfect Earth.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-rough-moon
An earthly-looking Moon

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-jupiter-moons
The moving points are understood as moons orbiting Jupiter. They show that not everything circles Earth directly, but do not alone prove that Earth moves.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-jupiter-moons
A system of moons around Jupiter

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-copernican-world
In Copernicus's model, Earth and the other planets orbit the Sun. Earth is a moving planet, not the stationary centre of the heavens.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-copernican-world
Earth as a moving planet

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-relative-retrograde
A planet can appear to move backward because Earth and that planet are both moving. Relative motion explains the appearance without requiring an actual reversal.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-relative-retrograde
Apparent reversal from relative motion

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-venus-phases
Repeated observations reveal Venus's full range of phases. Any successful model must account for both its crescent and nearly full appearances.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-venus-phases
The full range of Venus’s phases

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-tycho-still-live
Venus's phases can fit Tycho's model, in which the Sun circles a stationary Earth and the other planets circle the Sun. Those observations alone therefore do not prove that Earth moves.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-tycho-still-live
A stationary-Earth alternative remains live

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-kepler-ellipse
Kepler uses an ellipse rather than a perfect circle to describe Mars's orbit. A different shape fits the inherited observations better.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-kepler-ellipse
An elliptical path for Mars

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-revised-harmony
Kepler replaces the perfect circle with an ellipse for Mars's orbit. Astronomy can still seek lawful order while changing what that order looks like to fit the observations.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-revised-harmony
A lawful sky without perfect circles

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-working-natural-history
Bacon includes workshop practices in the study of nature. Changing materials can reveal things that passive observation misses.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-working-natural-history
Natural history includes what crafts can do

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-bias-under-examination
An inquiry deliberately looks for evidence that could challenge its preferred answer. This reduces one source of error without making the investigator free of bias.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-bias-under-examination
An inquiry that looks for unwelcome cases

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-heat-light-conjecture
Sunlight and flame suggest that heat may be connected with light. Two supporting examples are a starting proposal, not a general proof.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-heat-light-conjecture
Heat seems tied to light

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-light-not-sufficient
A flame gives light and warmth, but bright moonlight gives little noticeable warmth. This comparison challenges a simple link between visible brightness and felt heat; it does not show that moonlight transfers no energy.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-light-not-sufficient
Brightness need not bring comparable felt warmth

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-heat-beyond-light
Rubbing can produce warmth without a glowing source. An account that ties heat only to shining objects therefore misses an ordinary case and must be reconsidered.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-heat-beyond-light
Heat must be sought beyond luminous bodies

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-rubbing-intervention
The rubbed wood warms while the untouched comparison does not. Changing one condition helps test whether rubbing causes the difference.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-rubbing-intervention
Rubbing changes the warming in this comparison

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-melancholy-many-sources
Burton studies melancholy through medical traditions, literature and experience. His account combines different kinds of knowledge rather than reducing the subject to one cause.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-melancholy-many-sources
Melancholy across bodies, books and experience

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-public-trial
Another workshop can inspect the method and repeat the trial. Sharing the procedure makes checking possible, though disputes about trust can remain.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-public-trial
A trial another workshop can repeat

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-repeatable-wonder
Light passing through a small opening forms an upside-down image inside a dark box. Other people can inspect and repeat the setup to check whether it produces the same image.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-repeatable-wonder
An optical wonder with an inspectable arrangement

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mechanical-nature
Nature is investigated through the movement and arrangement of matter. This approach asks whether physical mechanisms can explain familiar effects.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mechanical-nature
Nature investigated through matter and motion

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-clockwork-figure
The mechanical figure uses linked gears to move. The sky model shows how a linkage can coordinate movement, while the figure provides a new use for that idea.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-clockwork-figure
A figure moved by a copied gear linkage

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-imitation-limit
A gear-driven figure can copy an animal's movement. That alone does not show feeding, growth, repair or sensation. Mechanical imitation and a complete explanation of life are different achievements.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-imitation-limit
Imitated motion does not establish a living animal

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-falling-moon
An orbit can be understood as continual falling around Earth. This connects motion in the sky with falling near the ground.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-falling-moon
An orbit considered as continual falling

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-universal-gravitation
Newton proposes that the same attraction accounts for falling bodies and planetary motion. The proposal connects observations of Earth and sky, rather than proving itself from a wish for harmony.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-universal-gravitation
Newton proposes one attraction for Earth and sky

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-white-light-components
A selected colour stays the same through a second prism, while reunited colours make white light. Newton uses these comparisons to show that glass separates components of white light rather than simply manufacturing new colours.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-white-light-components
White light contains differently refracted colours

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-one-way-circulation
Valves in veins direct blood toward the heart. Their operation supplies evidence for the return part of a circulation account rather than merely assuming that blood circulates.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-one-way-circulation
Venous valves direct blood back to the heart

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-blood-circuit
The heart pumps blood out through arteries, and vein valves direct blood back toward the heart. Harvey joins these directions into a circulation account, though he cannot yet see the tiny connections through tissues.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-blood-circuit
Out through arteries, back through veins

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-newtonian-framework
Newton describes motion on Earth and in the sky in one system. He assumes space exists independently of the objects in it, and time passes alike everywhere. Later theories must explain both the system's successes and its limits.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-newtonian-framework
Universal mechanics in absolute space and time

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-time-longitude
A difference between local time and reference time gives an east–west position difference. The method depends on a reliable reference.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-time-longitude
Longitude from two kinds of time

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-sea-tested-clock
The report describes how Harrison's H4 clock kept the time of a known place during a sea voyage. Comparing that reference time with local noon gives longitude: how far east or west the observer is from that place. The successful trial supports the method, but does not certify every clock. Further testing and the reward remained disputed.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-sea-tested-clock
Sea-tested reference time

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-lunar-reference
The Moon changes position against the stars as time passes. A navigator can compare its measured position with prepared tables to find the time at a known place.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-lunar-reference
Reference time read from the Moon

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-lunar-longitude
A navigator compares the Moon’s measured position with prepared tables to find the time at a known place. Comparing that time with local time gives the east–west position. This is an alternative to carrying a sea clock, but needs a clear view of the sky.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-lunar-longitude
A lunar route to longitude

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-jaipur-comparison
At Jaipur, different astronomical traditions are compared with observations. Inherited tables become material for active revision rather than unquestioned authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-jaipur-comparison
Astronomical traditions compared at Jaipur

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-masonry-observatory
Large fixed instruments are designed for measuring the sky and checking astronomical tables. Their scale and stable sightlines serve that work.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-masonry-observatory
Plan for a masonry observatory

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-encyclopedic-craft
Craft knowledge is included in a widely shared reference work. Practical making becomes part of learned knowledge despite censorship and disagreement.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-encyclopedic-craft
Craft enters the public account of knowledge

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-encyclopedic-network
Cross-references let readers connect different subjects and practices. The structure supports more than one route through knowledge.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-encyclopedic-network
Knowledge joined by cross-reference

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-problem-of-induction
A pattern that held in past observations is not logically guaranteed to hold in the next case. Hume asks what justifies making that further prediction.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-problem-of-induction
Past success does not guarantee the next case

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-conditions-of-experience
Hume questions what justifies extending observed patterns beyond the cases already seen. Kant responds by asking what the mind contributes to make experience possible. His account is a philosophical response, not one experiment that settles the dispute.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-conditions-of-experience
Kant: the mind helps structure experience

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-sufficiency-against-status
Social standing can demand more and more possessions. The older ideal of friendship and having enough challenges that demand: a higher status is not the same thing as a better life.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-sufficiency-against-status
Enough is not the same as higher status

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-universal-rights
Rights are claimed for people beyond inherited rank or privilege. Whether the claim is truly universal depends on whom law and practice include.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-universal-rights
A claim to rights beyond inherited rank

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-rights-for-women
A declaration may invoke universal reason while women are denied the education used to develop it. Wollstonecraft turns that stated principle against the exclusion and unequal treatment of women.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-rights-for-women
Universal reason must include women

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-rights-against-slavery
A claim of universal liberty conflicts with enslavement. The testimony and actions of enslaved people expose that conflict, rather than leaving it as an abstract puzzle.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-rights-against-slavery
Universal liberty confronts enslavement

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-public-reasoning
Arguments are opened to criticism from a wider public. Broader discussion can challenge authority without guaranteeing equal participation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-public-reasoning
An argument answerable to a wider public

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-private-vice-public-benefit
Mandeville argues that private motives condemned as vices can support public prosperity. The uncomfortable question is whether wealth created by such motives makes a society good.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-private-vice-public-benefit
Mandeville: private vice, public benefit

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-division-of-labour
Work is divided into specialized tasks, allowing more to be produced. Organization, not only machinery, can change a workshop's output.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-division-of-labour
Production divided into specialized tasks

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-specialization-cost
Specialized work may increase output while narrowing the worker's abilities and daily life. Greater efficiency is not the same as greater well-being.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-specialization-cost
More output can narrow the maker’s life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-prosperity-not-good-life
More trade and wealth do not automatically provide friendship, security or worthwhile activity. Comparing economic prosperity with the older ideal of a sufficient life keeps those different measures of success visible.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-prosperity-not-good-life
Prosperity and a good life can diverge

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-population-pressure
Malthus argues that potential population growth can exceed growth in food supplies. This can create pressure, but it does not decide which response is fair.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-population-pressure
More offspring than available subsistence

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-scarcity-and-justice
A shortage describes a problem; it does not decide who should bear its costs. A policy still needs a separate justification.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-scarcity-and-justice
Scarcity does not settle who should bear it

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-long-distance-exchange
Navigation and preserved food allow exchange over longer distances. Goods can travel farther because voyages can be located and sustained.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-long-distance-exchange
An enlarged world of exchange

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-coercive-exchange
Long-distance exchange can depend on forced labour and political power. Technical improvements alone do not explain who benefits or who suffers.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-coercive-exchange
Exchange entangled with coercion

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-work-as-a-human-question
Workers are people whose abilities and lives matter, not only sources of output. A productive arrangement can still fail them.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-work-as-a-human-question
The maker is more than the output

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-organic-fossil
The object is understood as a trace of former life. Comparing it with living organisms turns it into evidence about the past.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-organic-fossil
A fossil is a trace of former life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-relative-strata
Rock layers can be placed in order from earlier to later. This establishes a sequence without yet giving ages in years.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-relative-strata
Rock layers in order

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-correlated-strata
Matching fossil sequences connects separated rock layers. A history can be reconstructed across more than one exposed cliff.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-correlated-strata
Separated beds, a comparable succession

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-process-history
Streams erode rock and deposit sediment today. Those observed processes help explain similar traces preserved in ancient rock.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-process-history
Present processes can explain ancient traces

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cycles-in-rock
Tilted rock layers beneath younger level layers require several episodes: deposition, tilting, erosion and new deposition. The landscape records repeated change rather than one unchanged act of formation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cycles-in-rock
More than one making of the landscape

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-deep-time-world
Many layers and repeated changes require a long history. The evidence establishes an extended past without yet measuring exactly how many years it lasted.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-deep-time-world
A landscape needs a deep past

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-extinction-real
Some kinds of animals no longer exist. A catalogue of life must therefore include a past that differs from the present.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-extinction-real
Whole forms of life have disappeared

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-eventful-geology
Earth's history includes long-running processes and abrupt events. It need not be reduced to either constant slow change or one worldwide catastrophe.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-eventful-geology
Long processes and abrupt events

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-named-species
Shared two-part names make records of species easier to compare. The names help investigation without making the groups beyond revision.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-named-species
Comparable names for living kinds

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-geography-of-life
Living things vary with place, climate and altitude. Comparing these patterns connects classification with the conditions of life.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-geography-of-life
Life patterned by place and conditions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-transforming-species
Lamarck argues that use and disuse change an organism's body, and those changes can pass to its offspring. This differs from natural selection, which acts on inherited differences already present.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-transforming-species
Species can have changing histories

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-selection-analogy
Breeding shows how small inherited differences can accumulate. Natural selection uses the comparison without requiring a breeder who plans nature's changes.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-selection-analogy
Small inherited differences can accumulate

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-struggle-for-existence
Offspring have inherited differences and face limited resources. These facts alone do not show which differences will leave more descendants; reproductive outcomes must also be compared.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-struggle-for-existence
Heritably varied offspring face limited resources

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-natural-selection
Inherited differences that affect reproduction can become more or less common over generations. Natural selection describes this population change, not a rule that the strongest always win.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-natural-selection
Unequal reproduction changes a population

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cumulative-evolution
Small inherited changes can accumulate across many generations. Deep geological time gives that process a scale far beyond a human lifetime.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cumulative-evolution
Selection can reshape life across deep time

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-changed-meaning-of-kinds
Lamarck explains species change through bodily changes acquired during life and passed to offspring. Darwin and Wallace's account of natural selection instead concerns inherited differences that affect survival and reproduction. They agree that species change, but differ about how.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-changed-meaning-of-kinds
Changing species, different accounts of how

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-open-heredity
Evolutionary change requires inherited differences to persist across generations. How that happened remained unresolved in these nineteenth-century arguments.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-open-heredity
Cumulative evolution with heredity still unresolved

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-industrial-production
Powered machinery and specialized tasks increase the scale of production. The factory combines a technology with a way of organizing people.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-industrial-production
Specialized labour at machine scale

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-fuel-dependent-industry
Industrial output depends on a continuing fuel supply. Its apparent power has material costs and consequences.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-fuel-dependent-industry
Industrial power has a material appetite

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-alienated-work
In Marx's critique, workers can lose control over their activity and its products. The problem is a relationship of ownership and power, not simply disliking a task.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-alienated-work
The maker can lose control of the work

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-useful-pleasurable-work
Morris proposes work that produces useful things while giving the maker worthwhile activity. It is an alternative to assess, not a completed solution to economic organization.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-useful-pleasurable-work
Useful work need not erase the maker

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cooperation-in-evolution
Cooperation can help organisms survive and reproduce. Recognizing its importance does not refute natural selection.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cooperation-in-evolution
Mutual aid belongs inside the question of survival

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-competitive-story-limited
Nature includes cooperation as well as competition. Neither fact by itself tells people how a society ought to be governed.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-competitive-story-limited
Nature is not a competition-only morality tale

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-control-of-work
A labour-saving machine may still leave workers with less say over their pace and decisions. Reduced effort and greater control are separate gains.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-control-of-work
Mechanized effort can deepen lost control

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-microscopic-life
The microscope reveals living things too small for unaided sight. Discovering them expands knowledge without automatically explaining which ones cause illness.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-microscopic-life
A previously unseen living world

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-waterborne-pattern
Cholera deaths differ between comparable households using different water supplies. This supports investigating water as a cause while still checking other differences and the quality of records.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-waterborne-pattern
Cholera mortality tracks a water-supply difference

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-prevention-before-settlement
The cholera comparison points toward contaminated water as a preventable route of disease. Avoiding that water can be justified before everyone agrees about the complete microscopic cause.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-prevention-before-settlement
A useful intervention before full agreement

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-competing-disease-accounts
One account blames cholera on foul air; another connects it with contaminated water. Comparing the water-supply evidence challenges the rival explanations without pretending they have equal support.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-competing-disease-accounts
Foul air or a waterborne cause?

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-microbial-growth
Air reaches both broths, but dust reaches only one, and only that broth develops growth. The comparison supports microorganisms arriving with dust rather than air alone producing life.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-microbial-growth
Broth growth tracks incoming microorganisms

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-living-fermentation
Living cultures participate in changes such as fermentation. Biological activity becomes part of the explanation of material transformation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-living-fermentation
Organisms participate in chemical change

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-chemical-agency-in-cells
Yeast extract ferments even without intact yeast cells. Buchner's later result shows that some of the chemistry can operate outside a whole living cell, revising the earlier claim without erasing microorganisms' role.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-chemical-agency-in-cells
Living activity includes separable chemistry

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-electromagnetic-link
An electric current makes a nearby compass needle turn. This shows that electricity can have a magnetic effect without a visible mechanical push.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-electromagnetic-link
Electricity acts magnetically

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-induction
Changing a magnetic field can produce a current. The effect provides a way to generate electrical action.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-induction
A changing field can produce a current

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-field-description
A field describes how physical influence varies throughout space. The surroundings become part of the explanation, not just empty distance between objects.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-field-description
The surrounding space becomes part of the account

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-electromagnetic-light
The linked electrical and magnetic account predicts waves travelling at light's speed. Light becomes part of the same physical theory.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-electromagnetic-light
Light belongs to the electromagnetic field

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-networked-time
A reference time can be sent by electrical signals to distant clocks. People no longer have to rely only on carrying a clock between places.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-networked-time
Clock coordination travels over a network

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-standard-time
Railway stations adopt a shared time so that timetables agree. That practical standard differs from each place's own solar time.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-standard-time
A shared railway time

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-photographic-trace
Light produces a lasting image on a prepared surface. The image records an exposure, but still requires choices about the setup and interpretation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-photographic-trace
Light leaves a lasting image

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-framed-photograph
A photograph records the view chosen by the photographer. Its physical connection to the scene does not make everything outside the frame visible.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-framed-photograph
A photograph records from a selected viewpoint

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-reproducible-image
Many prints can be made from one negative. Wider circulation changes who can see the image without making its framing neutral.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-reproducible-image
The image can circulate in many copies

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-inner-continuity
William James describes experience as a changing but connected flow. Attention shifts while earlier thoughts continue to shape the next moment.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-inner-continuity
Experience has a moving continuity

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-lived-duration
An experience has qualities and continuity that a sequence of clock readings does not describe. This does not make clocks useless or inaccurate.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-lived-duration
Lived time is not a row of clock units

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-interpreted-dream
Freud's approach reads a dream for hidden wishes and conflicts. It is an interpretive proposal, not the same kind of result as a physical measurement.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-interpreted-dream
A dream can be read for concealed conflict

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-automatic-writing
The writer loosens ordinary planning to follow words and associations. Surrealists use this as an artistic experiment, not a verified diagnosis.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-automatic-writing
Writing without the usual planned control

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-surreal-encounter
An unexpected combination makes familiar things seem strange. The artwork asks the viewer to reconsider ordinary associations and expectations.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-surreal-encounter
The familiar becomes strangely connected

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-art-beyond-illustration
An artwork can raise questions about ordinary life and society. It need not merely illustrate an already finished theory.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-art-beyond-illustration
An artwork can challenge ordinary life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-plural-access-to-time
Railway clocks coordinate public events, while descriptions of lived time address memory and experience. These answer different questions; a remembered melody does not refute a working clock.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-plural-access-to-time
Measured coordination and lived duration

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-frame-dependent-simultaneity
Two flashes happen at a moving train's ends at the same time according to platform clocks, but their light reaches a passenger at the train's midpoint at different times. For the passenger, light travels equal distances from the two ends at the same speed. The passenger therefore concludes that the flashes happened at different times.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-frame-dependent-simultaneity
Different frames need not agree on distant simultaneity

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-special-relativity
Observers moving steadily past one another use the same physical laws and measure the same speed of light. They can still disagree about whether far-apart events happened at the same time. Newton's mechanics remains a useful approximation at everyday speeds.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-special-relativity
No universal time for distant events

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-newtonian-limit
Newton treats time as passing the same way for everyone. Relativity says moving observers can disagree about whether distant events happen at the same time. Newton's account still works well at everyday speeds.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-newtonian-limit
Newton’s shared time is a useful limit, not universal

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-local-equivalence
A person in a freely falling room can locally feel weightless. Einstein uses the relation between gravity and acceleration to motivate a new account; this does not make gravitational differences vanish everywhere.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-local-equivalence
Free fall connects gravity and acceleration

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-geometric-gravity
In Einstein's account, matter and the geometry of space and time affect the paths bodies follow. Gravity is no longer described only as a force acting across fixed space.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-geometric-gravity
Gravity changes the geometry of paths

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-tested-light-bending
Einstein's gravitational theory predicts a change in the path of light near the Sun. Eclipse observations test that consequence, with measurement uncertainty and interpretation still part of the evidence.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-tested-light-bending
A new geometry meets a light observation

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-reassembled-continents
Continents can be placed in a proposed earlier arrangement and compared. The reconstruction must fit evidence, not just look like a puzzle.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-reassembled-continents
Continents can be compared in an earlier arrangement

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-continental-drift-case
Matching fossils and rock formations strengthen the case that separated continents share a past. Several kinds of evidence matter more than an outline resemblance alone.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-continental-drift-case
Separated lands share a geological past

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-drift-without-drive
Matching fossils and rocks suggest that continents once joined have moved apart, but Wegener did not have a convincing account of the force moving them. Evidence that they moved can be stronger than the current explanation of how.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-drift-without-drive
A strong pattern, an unresolved driving account

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mineral-clock
Radioactive decay can turn a mineral into a measure of elapsed time. Interpreting it requires checking whether later events changed the material.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mineral-clock
A rock can contain a measurable clock

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-absolute-age-possible
Rock layers give an order of events, but radioactive decay supplies a measure of elapsed time. Mineral evidence can now address how many years passed, provided its later history is understood.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-absolute-age-possible
The sequence can acquire durations

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-seafloor-spreading
New ocean floor forms at ridges and spreads out on both sides. As its rock cools, it records Earth's magnetic direction. Matching bands on opposite sides of a ridge show that the floor has moved outward.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-seafloor-spreading
Ocean floor is made and moves away from ridges

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mobile-plates
Continents move as parts of rigid plates made of crust and the uppermost mantle. Plate tectonics is not simply Wegener's original mechanism declared correct.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mobile-plates
Continents move with plates of rigid outer Earth

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-recycled-crust
Plates form, move and descend at different boundaries. These processes fit together without requiring Earth to grow steadily larger.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-recycled-crust
Plates form, move and descend

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-relocated-habitats
Continents move as parts of plates, changing the locations and connections of habitats. Patterns of living things must therefore be explained on a changing surface rather than a fixed map.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-relocated-habitats
Life’s changing map includes moving land

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-boolean-logic
Logical relationships can be represented and manipulated with symbols. The form of an argument becomes something that can be worked on systematically.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-boolean-logic
Inference becomes a manipulable symbolic system

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-logic-circuit
A circuit carries out logical operations through switching. Correct operation does not prove that the information supplied to it is true.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-logic-circuit
Logical operations become switching actions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-mechanical-procedure
A symbol machine follows explicit rules for reading, writing and moving through its work. The procedure can be described separately from the particular machine that carries it out.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-mechanical-procedure
A rule-governed symbol machine

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-universal-computation
One machine can follow many procedures when their instructions are encoded as data. It is not limited to one permanently built-in task.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-universal-computation
One machine can follow many encoded procedures

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-computation-limit
No program can always work out whether any other program will eventually stop or run forever. This limits one universal test, not the usefulness of programs in general.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-computation-limit
No procedure solves every halting case

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-communication-problem
A transmitted signal can lose distinctions needed to recover the message. Communication design must protect those distinctions, not just send something detectable.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-communication-problem
A transmitted message can lose distinctions

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-error-correcting-message
The message includes enough structured extra information to recover from the specified error patterns. It cannot repair every possible corruption or prove that the message is true.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-error-correcting-message
A message designed to recover from specified errors

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-feedback-control
The machine uses measurements of its effects to adjust what it does. Responding to feedback does not by itself establish understanding or unlimited authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-feedback-control
A machine responds to its measured effects

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-word-identical-page
In Borges's imagined case, comparing the later page with Cervantes confirms that every word matches. That settles the wording while leaving questions about authorship and interpretation open.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-word-identical-page
The collation finds the same words

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-another-work
In Borges's imagined case, identical words acquire a different meaning through a later author and context. This does not mean every interpretation is equally well supported.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-another-work
The same words can become another work

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-useless-total-map
A map that reproduces everything at full size loses the usefulness of selection. More detail is not always a better guide.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-useless-total-map
More detail can destroy a map’s usefulness

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-branching-fiction
A story can keep incompatible paths open instead of selecting one ending. Its branching resembles a diagram of ancestry without being the same kind of explanation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-branching-fiction
A narrative can keep incompatible paths open

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-fictional-archive
A work of fiction can imitate editions, notes and references. Scholarly appearance alone is not enough to authenticate a claim.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-fictional-archive
The apparatus of scholarship becomes fiction

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-aura-and-reproduction
A copy changes how and where a work is encountered. Reproduction raises questions about presence, tradition and access beyond the technical ability to make copies.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-aura-and-reproduction
A copy changes the encounter with a work

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-politics-of-the-copy
Reproduction can extend art's audience and alter its political uses. Those changes cannot all be reduced to either liberation or decline.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-politics-of-the-copy
Reproduction changes art’s political reach

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-genealogy-of-values
Moral language has a history shaped by conflict and power. Investigating that history raises questions without automatically deciding whether a value is justified.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-genealogy-of-values
A moral vocabulary has a contested history

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-relational-categories
A category gains meaning through its relations to other categories. A shared name alone does not explain its role in a social system.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-relational-categories
A category gets meaning from a pattern of relations

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-knowledge-and-discipline
Classifications can be used to guide, train or control people. The effect depends on specific institutions and practices, not knowledge in the abstract.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-knowledge-and-discipline
A classification can help organize conduct

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-false-natural-hierarchy
Racial classifications can present domination as a natural order. Exposing that error does not make all biological naming and comparison useless.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-false-natural-hierarchy
A classification can naturalize domination

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-unequal-universalism
An account can claim to include all people while excluding women or ranking racial groups as inferior. Comparing these cases exposes a gap between universal claims and practice without treating their histories as interchangeable.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-unequal-universalism
A universal promise can coexist with exclusion

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-unstable-distinction
An imagined critic says only originals create meaning, yet praises a word-identical later page as new. The critic's own example grants a copy the creative role the argument denies it. This identifies a particular tension, not a flaw in every text.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-unstable-distinction
The original/copy hierarchy unsettles its own case

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-situated-evidence
Evidence comes from particular positions and practices, so every view has limits. A limited view can still be constrained by what is actually there.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-situated-evidence
A view can be partial without being arbitrary

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-accountable-perspectives
Different viewpoints can be compared with evidence and corrected. Recognizing partial knowledge does not require treating all claims as equally good.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-accountable-perspectives
Partial perspectives can still answer to evidence

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-particulate-heredity
Distinct inherited factors can persist across generations rather than blending away. This gives an answer to the older difficulty of how variation can survive long enough for selection to act.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-particulate-heredity
Inheritance need not blend differences away

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-molecular-heredity
DNA provides a physical carrier for inherited differences. A molecular mechanism adds detail without explaining every trait on its own.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-molecular-heredity
The inherited difference has a molecular carrier

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-genes-in-context
Inherited information works through development and environmental conditions. Understanding an organism requires more than reading its genes alone.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-genes-in-context
Inherited information acts in a developing world

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-ecosystem-relations
A habitat is a network of interacting organisms. Where they live matters together with how they feed, compete and cooperate.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-ecosystem-relations
A habitat is a network of living relations

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-carbon-energy-link
Fossil-fuel energy transfers long-stored carbon into the atmosphere. Industrial power therefore changes a planetary store of material.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-carbon-energy-link
Industrial power changes the atmospheric store

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-radiative-warming
Earth's surface gives off heat as infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit some of it, reducing the heat escaping directly to space. The surface and lower atmosphere warm until outgoing heat again balances incoming sunlight.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-radiative-warming
Added greenhouse gas changes Earth’s heat loss

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-climate-pressure
Fossil-fuel emissions and greenhouse physics together explain a warming influence. The explanation is more than a correlation between two rising records.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-climate-pressure
The energy system creates a warming pressure

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-atmospheric-history
Bubbles in dated ice preserve samples of past air. Comparing them over time gives a history of atmospheric composition, unlike a sample whose age is unknown.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-atmospheric-history
The air has a readable history

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-human-driven-warming
Human activity drives recent global warming through greenhouse emissions and other influences. Uncertainty about some details does not erase the physical mechanism or the wider evidence.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-human-driven-warming
Human activity drives the recent warming

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-clean-measurement-problem
Modern lead contamination can distort measurements intended to date ancient material. The sample's own lead must be separated from what the laboratory adds.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-clean-measurement-problem
The laboratory can contaminate an ancient clock

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-world-history-not-destiny
Geography and ecology affect what societies can do, but do not determine every event. Institutions, forced labour, individual choices and unexpected events also shape history.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-world-history-not-destiny
Geography constrains history without writing it alone

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-predictive-assistant
The imagined model is useful at finding patterns but is not an all-purpose source of truth. Its errors and limits remain part of the design.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-predictive-assistant
A powerful predictor, not a universal oracle

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-trial-design-partner
The model proposes a test with a method people can inspect. A useful suggestion must expose what changes, what is measured and how it can be checked.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-trial-design-partner
A model proposes an experiment that people can inspect

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-provenance-rich-observatory
The observatory keeps records of how each finding was made. Inspectable evidence matters more than a polished appearance of authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-provenance-rich-observatory
A record whose making can be examined

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-uncertainty-bearing-model
In the imagined observatory, a new case differs from the examples used to train the model. The prediction keeps that unfamiliarity visible instead of hiding it behind confident language.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-uncertainty-bearing-model
A prediction keeps its unfamiliar case visible

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-cross-checked-discovery
Differently designed instruments detect the same candidate event and pass local checks. Their agreement strengthens the finding, but shared errors or disturbances may still explain it.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-cross-checked-discovery
A candidate survives distinct checks

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-remote-observation-agent
The observatory can adjust its observations while waiting for Earth. Feedback supports a limited local role, not unlimited machine authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-remote-observation-agent
A local observer can act while Earth is silent

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-bounded-autonomy
The system can act locally within an agreed boundary and stop for review beyond it. Responsiveness and oversight are both built into its role.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-bounded-autonomy
Useful local action with a defined stopping boundary

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-automation-with-agency
The workshop combines machinery with specific rights to choose tasks and stop unsafe work. Community ownership alone would not guarantee those powers.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-automation-with-agency
A workshop design reserves decisions for its makers

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-workshop-for-living
The workshop values skill and the maker's experience as well as output. Its design does not promise that every necessary task becomes enjoyable.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-workshop-for-living
A workshop can value skill as well as output

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-care-still-needed
The imagined workshop offers skill and pleasure in making, but care and maintenance remain demanding. A good-life proposal still has to decide how necessary work is supported and shared.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-care-still-needed
Pleasurable making does not eliminate care burdens

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-responsive-city
The imagined city uses local signals to respond to urgent problems. Unlike a remote observatory, its choices directly involve conflicting human priorities.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-responsive-city
A city can respond locally to urgent signals

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-control-tradeoff
An imagined city controller can react quickly to danger, but affected residents may disagree with its priorities. Faster action leaves less time for challenge before the consequences occur.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-control-tradeoff
Faster response can reduce time to challenge it

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-contestable-control
The imagined city gives residents a way to inspect and appeal rapid decisions. This adds accountability, but cannot undo every harm or recover the time lost before an appeal is heard.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-contestable-control
Rapid action has an inspectable appeal path

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-revisable-commons
Cooperation is supported by rules that affected people can change. Willingness to cooperate alone does not design a workable institution.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-revisable-commons
Cooperation receives rules people can revise

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-plural-good-lives
A prosperous city need not require everyone to pursue the same life. Wealth is one consideration, not a complete definition of living well.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-plural-good-lives
A prosperous city need not prescribe one good life

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-common-good
Political life can be judged by what it enables people to share and achieve. Asking about a common good also raises the question of who is included.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-common-good
Political life judged by a common good

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-rule-benefit
A government is judged by whose interests it serves, not only by how many people rule. A ruler's benefit can differ from the community's.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-rule-benefit
Judge rulers by whose interests they serve

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-citizen-boundary
Living in a city does not necessarily give a person a political voice. Ancient citizenship excluded many people on whom the city depended.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-citizen-boundary
Living in a city does not grant a political voice

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-bounded-common-good
A city can claim to serve a common good while denying many inhabitants a political voice. Whose good is included, and whose interests are left out?

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-bounded-common-good
Who is included in the common good?

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-skilled-rule
Plato argues that ruling requires knowledge of what is good, not simply winning support. The claim does not settle who should be allowed a political voice.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-skilled-rule
Plato: ruling needs knowledge, not applause

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-unequal-status
Roman law distinguishes freeborn, freed and enslaved people. Those legal statuses give people different rights and are not the same as citizenship or participation in government in a Greek city.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-unequal-status
A different legal order still divides people by status

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-law-nature-tension
Justinian's legal collection describes slavery as contrary to nature while continuing to regulate it. A stated principle therefore conflicts with the institution that the same law upholds.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-law-nature-tension
Law upholds what it calls contrary to nature

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-status-changed
Roman manumission legally changes an individual from enslaved to freed status. The institution of slavery can remain in place even though this person's status changes.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-status-changed
An enslaved person becomes a freed person

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-reform-not-abolition
A route for freeing individuals leaves slavery as an institution in place. Relief within a system differs from ending the system.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-reform-not-abolition
An individual route to freedom is not abolition

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-authoritative-lawbook
The emperor's collection selects which older law is authoritative. Unlike an edition that displays variants, it also determines which text has legal force.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-authoritative-lawbook
Older law edited under imperial authority

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-interpreted-law
Medieval scholars at Bologna annotate Justinian's older law. Their commentary gives it new uses and meanings rather than simply continuing the sixth-century empire unchanged.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-interpreted-law
Inherited Roman law gains a medieval commentary

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-protection-purpose
Hobbes makes protection a central purpose of political authority. This answers the question of political benefit differently from the ancient accounts.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-protection-purpose
Hobbes makes protection a purpose of common power

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-hobbes-authority
Hobbes argues that authorized sovereign power can secure peace. The argument does not prove that concentrated power always succeeds.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-hobbes-authority
Hobbes: authorized sovereign power secures peace

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-protection-boundary
Hobbes grounds political obedience in the sovereign's power to protect. If that protection is lost, the subject's duty can end. Merely disliking a decision is not the same condition.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-protection-boundary
Hobbes: lost protection can end obedience

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-consent-government
Locke grounds legitimate government in consent. Sharing humanity is not itself the same as consenting to a particular political authority.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-consent-government
Locke: legitimate government rests on consent

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-resistance
Locke argues that a government's breach of trust can justify resistance. This does not guarantee success or imply that everyone then had an equal vote.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-resistance
Locke: breach of trust can justify resistance

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-rival-contracts
Hobbes emphasizes authorized power and protection; Locke emphasizes consent, trust and resistance to its breach. They offer different limits on authority, not one agreed social-contract doctrine.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-rival-contracts
Protection and resistance yield rival accounts of authority

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-emancipation-demand
Enslaved insurgents make freedom an active political demand. They are agents of change, not passive recipients of European ideas.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-emancipation-demand
Insurgents make freedom a political demand

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-emancipation-enacted
During the revolution in Saint-Domingue, uprising, war and political pressure lead to abolition decrees. Emancipation becomes law, while enforcement and freedom in daily life remain further questions.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-emancipation-enacted
Emancipation becomes law amid revolution

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-abolition-in-empire
The 1801 constitution rejects slavery but retains Saint-Domingue's connection with France. Abolition and independence are therefore distinct political achievements.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-abolition-in-empire
Abolition is claimed inside the French empire

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-work-consent
Compulsory plantation work prevents people from freely choosing their work. It puts the ideal of consent in conflict with the actual working arrangement.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-work-consent
Compulsory cultivation challenges consent

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-freedom-practice
Saint-Domingue's 1801 constitution rejects slavery while maintaining compulsory plantation work. This exposes a limit on freedom at work without erasing the achievement of emancipation.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-freedom-practice
Abolition coexists with coercive labour

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-independent-abolition
Haiti's independence breaks France's claim to rule and defends emancipation. It does not remove every form of coercion or secure immediate international recognition.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-independent-abolition
An independent state defends emancipation

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-civ-rights-in-practice
Saint-Domingue's abolition did not automatically end compulsory work. The imagined shared-resource institution therefore needs a way for people to challenge coercive rules, not just a formal promise of freedom.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-civ-rights-in-practice
A commons must let people challenge coercive rules

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-linear-perspective
Perspective represents a scene from one fixed eye position. It can make a convincing view without showing the whole place.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-linear-perspective
A single-viewpoint perspective

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-bearing-chart
The chart helps a navigator follow compass directions. It does not preserve every area's size and shape.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-bearing-chart
A chart made for compass bearings

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder body / plain-atlas-unequal-passenger-reception
Two flashes occur at a train's ends at the same time according to platform clocks. A passenger at the moving train's midpoint travels toward one flash and away from the other, so their light reaches the passenger at different times. These arrival times alone do not yet show when the flashes happened for the passenger.

## atlas-focused-sessions-v6 / wonder italic caption / plain-atlas-unequal-passenger-reception
The moving midpoint receives platform-simultaneous flashes separately

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / chooser / elements-basket-continuity-v10
Elements: A River Worth Returning To

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / chooser / elements-basket-continuity-v10
Starting from four storybook elements, travel from an orchard to a coastal gathering. Make useful things for people you meet. The world remembers what you leave behind.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / earth
Earth

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / earth
Loose ground containing mineral grains and clay. Plants can grow in suitable soil.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / water
Water

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / water
A liquid that flows, soaks materials and takes the shape of its container.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / air
Air

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / air
The gases around us. Moving air can carry moisture, fill a sail and supply oxygen to a flame.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / fire
Fire

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / fire
Burning produces heat and light. Heat can cook food and change materials.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / clay
Clay

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / clay
Soft, fine earth that holds an impressed shape while wet.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / stone
Stone

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / stone
Hard, rough mineral material; its weight and abrasive surfaces can do work.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / bowl
Formed clay bowl

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / bowl
A shaped, dried clay bowl that has not been fired. It can serve as a dry form, but is not ready to hold water.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / brick
Brick

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / brick
A hard, regular block of fired clay, made for stacking into lasting structures.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / rain
Rain

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / rain
Water arriving from the sky, enough to soak the ground rather than fill a vessel neatly.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / plant
Plant

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / plant
A living stem with roots and leaves; a source of several different natural materials.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / grain
Grain

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / grain
Dry edible seeds with a tough outer surface and a starchy interior.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / flour
Flour

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / flour
Fine meal ground from grain. Loose and powdery until it is wetted.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / dough
Dough

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / dough
A soft mass of flour and water. It can hold its shape, but has not yet risen or baked.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / flatbread
Flatbread

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / flatbread
A simple cooked bread without a raised, airy crumb.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / fruit
Fruit

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / fruit
Ripe, juicy produce with flesh around its seeds.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / juice
Fruit juice

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / juice
The fruit's liquid, separated from its flesh; sweet enough to feed fermentation.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sand
Sand

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sand
Small, hard mineral grains. Loose when poured, abrasive when rubbed.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / glass
Glass

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / glass
A hard, clear material that lets light through but needs shaping to give it a particular job.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / wood
Wood

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / wood
Rough timber: solid lengths with grain and bulk, not flat boards yet.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / window
Window

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / window
A pane held in a wooden frame, admitting light while closing an opening.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / fiber
Plant fiber

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / fiber
Flexible strands taken from plants. They can be spun into thread or made into sheets.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / thread
Thread

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / thread
A long, fine strand spun from fibers; useful for binding and stitching.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / cloth
Cloth

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / cloth
A flexible sheet made from interlaced threads.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / rope
Rope

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / rope
Thick cord made to hold loads or tie larger pieces together.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sleeping-mat
Sleeping mat

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sleeping-mat
A padded woven surface that separates a sleeper from the cold ground.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / house
House

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / house
A small weatherproof home with solid walls and a timber roof. A window admits daylight; a sleeping mat makes a place to rest. A stone hearth can warm the room.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / axe
Axe

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / axe
A sharp stone edge on a handle. Its job is to cut and shape wood, not to pound it.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / planks
Planks

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / planks
Flat, cut boards with broad faces and straight edges; easier to join into surfaces than rough timber.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / footbridge
Footbridge

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / footbridge
A narrow deck spanning the brook, supported and held together above the water.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / wheel
Wheel

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / wheel
A solid wooden disc, shaped to roll or turn around its centre. Its rim is still smooth.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / axle
Wheelset

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / axle
Wheels mounted on an axle. They can roll, but still need a body to carry a load.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / handcart
Handcart

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / handcart
A small rolling platform for moving a load by hand.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / waterwheel
Waterwheel

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / waterwheel
A paddled wheel that moving water can turn. Its turning force still needs a connection to useful work.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / millstone
Millstone

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / millstone
A heavy circular grinding stone. It can crush grain, but will not turn by itself.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / wooden-gear
Wooden gear

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / wooden-gear
A wooden wheel with teeth that transfer turning motion to another part.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / mill-gearing
Mill gearing

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / mill-gearing
A connected drive taking rotation from the waterwheel toward the grinding machinery.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / grain-mill
Grain mill

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / grain-mill
A water-driven grinding machine built for the village's daily grain, not just a single handful.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sack
Sack

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sack
A stitched cloth bag, closed at the sides and open at the top.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / flour-sack
Flour sack

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / flour-sack
A sack filled with flour, ready to take to the baker.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / starter
Wild starter

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / starter
A culture of microorganisms in flour and water. Given time, it produces gas that helps dough rise.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / risen-dough
Risen dough

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / risen-dough
Dough expanded by gas from fermentation, with bubbles held inside it.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / bread
Bread

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / bread
A baked loaf with an airy crumb and a firm crust.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / basket
Basket

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / basket
A woven plant-fiber carrier with a rope-bound rim and handle.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / picnic-basket
Picnic basket

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / picnic-basket
A basket packed with bread for a journey.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / jam
Jam

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / jam
Fruit cooked down into a thick spread; it still needs a vessel for keeping.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / jar
Clay jar

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / jar
A hollow clay container with a broad mouth, shaped using the bowl as a form.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / jar-lid
Jar lid

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / jar-lid
A fitted wooden cover for the mouth of a jar.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / preserve-jar
Preserve jar

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / preserve-jar
A jar filled with fruit preserve, but not yet covered.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sealed-preserves
Sealed preserves

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sealed-preserves
Fruit preserve put away in a covered jar for later meals.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / cider
Cider

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / cider
Fruit juice changed by fermentation into an alcoholic drink.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / glaze
Glaze

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / glaze
A coating that forms a smooth, glass-like surface when pottery is fired.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / glazed-bottle
Glazed clay jar

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / glazed-bottle
The same clay jar after coating and firing, with a smooth glassy surface. Its broad mouth and ceramic body remain unchanged.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / cider-bottle
Cider jar

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / cider-bottle
The glazed clay jar filled with cider and closed for carrying.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / kiln
Kiln

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / kiln
An enclosed firing chamber that concentrates heat around pottery or other materials.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / tile
Tile

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / tile
A flat, regular piece of shaped clay for covering a surface.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / glazed-bowl
Fired glazed bowl

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / glazed-bowl
A fired bowl with a smooth, finished surface rather than bare porous clay.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / table
Table

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / table
A sturdy raised wooden surface for preparing, displaying or serving things.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / tiled-table
Tiled table

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / tiled-table
A table with a durable tiled top, easy to wipe after a shared meal.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / hammer
Stone hammer

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / hammer
A hard, rounded stone held in the hand for breaking and pounding; it has no handle.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / ore
Iron ore

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / ore
Rock containing iron-bearing minerals, not usable metal yet.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / bellows
Bellows

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / bellows
A flexible air chamber that pushes a directed flow into a fire.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / forge
Forge

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / forge
A metalworker's hot hearth, with bellows to supply air and tools for shaping the metal.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / iron
Iron

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / iron
A metal that can be shaped into tools, fasteners and fittings.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / pickaxe
Pickaxe

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / pickaxe
A pointed metal head on a handle, suited to breaking hard ground and rock.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / carving-tool
Carving tool

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / carving-tool
A sharpened metal cutting tool for careful shaping rather than broad chopping.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / nails
Nails

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / nails
Pointed metal fasteners; their stems can also serve as small pins.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / hinge
Hinge

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / hinge
A joint that lets a door or lid swing while staying attached.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / wooden-chest
Wooden chest

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / wooden-chest
A box assembled from boards, with room to protect things inside.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / raft
Raft

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / raft
Timbers bound into a floating platform, without a sail.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sail
Sail

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sail
A broad sheet rigged to catch the wind and pull on its lines.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / sailboat
Sailboat

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / sailboat
A small floating craft able to use the wind for movement.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / pier-post
Pier post

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / pier-post
A stout prepared post, ready to support a landing above the water.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / dock
Dock

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / dock
A fixed landing where a boat can meet the shore.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / charcoal
Charcoal

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / charcoal
A carbon-rich fuel made by heating wood with limited air.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / torch
Oil-wick torch

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / torch
An oil-soaked fiber head secured to a wooden support; a small portable flame, not loose charcoal balanced on timber.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / lantern
Lantern

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / lantern
A light protected by a clear enclosure rather than left as an exposed flame.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / tower
Tower

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / reader-body / tower
A tall masonry structure with an opening to look out from above its surroundings.

## elements-basket-continuity-v10 / tile/reader-caption / lighthouse
