The travelling printer takes an impression from the delivered block: RIVER →, like the paper master. The same design has served two different commissions.

## copy-workroom-locations-v2 / story body / printer-commission
The next customer needs a block for printing. People will read its paper impression, not the wooden face, so the carved design must account for that reversal.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / chooser / divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7
Divinity: Disputed Questions

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / chooser / divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7
A manuscript desk, not a test of faith. Follow a wound, a failed promise, bread, a hungry household and a request for prayer through changing arguments. Build what each writer is claiming, test it against a case, and leave real disagreements open. New chapters reuse distinctions you have made; Read offers short explanations, not a hidden recipe book.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / cut-body
Case: a cut body has lost its health

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / cut-body
Imagine an injured person. The body remains, but it is not as sound as it should be. Nothing here says that the injured person deserves the injury.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / healed-body
Case: the wound heals; no evil thing escapes

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / healed-body
The injury disappears as the body heals. This is Augustine’s kind of example: the loss is repaired, rather than a second material being removed.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / stolen-meal
Case: I take the meal my neighbour needs

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / stolen-meal
Imagine taking another person’s share for your own comfort, knowing whose share it is. This is a deliberate action, unlike merely suffering a wound.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / fair-share
Comfort is good; my neighbour is still owed food

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / fair-share
Enjoying food is good, but the neighbour is still owed their share. The wrong is taking that food, not enjoying a meal.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / creation-good
Genesis: creation was “very good”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / creation-good
Augustine accepts the goodness of creation as a religious starting point. The wound example does not establish that starting point.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / health-lost
A wound is missing health, not an extra substance

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / health-lost
The body is still there, but its health has been damaged. Calling the wound a loss does not make its pain unreal.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / choice-unfair
The wrong is taking what another is owed

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / choice-unfair
Enjoying food and being able to choose are not themselves wrong. The wrong choice is taking food that another person is owed.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / harm-not-guilt
Being hurt is not the same as doing wrong

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / harm-not-guilt
A wound is harm suffered; stealing the meal is a wrong deliberately chosen. Comparing them as losses must not turn suffering into evidence of guilt.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / augustine-loss
Augustine: evil is good damaged or missing, not separate stuff

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / augustine-loss @ divinity-augustine-study
evil is good damaged or missing, not separate stuff

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / augustine-loss @ divinity-grace-alcove
evil is good damaged or missing, not separate stuff

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / augustine-loss
Augustine thinks evil is damage to, or loss of, something good rather than a separate evil material. A wound illustrates the idea: the body remains, but health is missing. His belief in a good creation is a starting point, not something the example proves.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / known-duty
Case: I know I should return the stolen meal

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / known-duty
The person understands the duty. More information about who owns the meal would not address every part of the problem.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / failed-promise
Case: I promise fairness, then choose comfort again

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / failed-promise
The person knows they should return the food and promises to do so, then chooses comfort again. Understanding and promising have not yet produced the right action.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / romans-line
Paul: “The good that I would I do not”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / romans-line
Here “would” means “want to”. Augustine reads the passage as a challenge to accounts of help that stop at ability or teaching.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / love-help
Augustine: God can stir love, not merely teach

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / love-help @ divinity-augustine-study
God can stir love, not merely teach

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / love-help @ divinity-grace-alcove
God can stir love, not merely teach

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / love-help
Augustine distinguishes knowing what is right from wanting and choosing it. He calls God's inward help with that choice grace.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / baby-no-act
Case: this infant has chosen no wrongful act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / baby-no-act
The case deliberately removes personal acts. If the infant still needs forgiveness, the explanation must concern something besides an act the infant chose.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / baby-baptism
Augustine’s church baptizes infants for forgiveness

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / baby-baptism
Augustine's church baptizes infants for forgiveness. Because an infant has chosen no wrongful act, Augustine asks what this forgiveness addresses.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / adam-condition
Augustine: we inherit a sinful condition from the first human, Adam

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / adam-condition @ divinity-augustine-study
we inherit a sinful condition from the first human, Adam

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / adam-condition @ divinity-grace-alcove
we inherit a sinful condition from the first human, Adam

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / adam-condition
Adam is the first human in the biblical story. Augustine says Adam's disobedience leaves later humans needing forgiveness even before choosing a wrong act. For him, an infant's illness does not show that the child has behaved badly.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / knowing-doing-gap
I know what is right, but still choose otherwise

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / knowing-doing-gap
Knowing what is right has not been enough to make the person do it. Augustine argues that God's help must address willing and acting as well as ignorance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / aided-choice
Augustine: God helps the person want and act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / aided-choice @ divinity-augustine-study
God helps the person want and act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / aided-choice @ divinity-grace-alcove
God helps the person want and act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / aided-choice
Augustine says God can help a person want and do what is right. The person still chooses and acts; help does not make the human actor disappear.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / infant-question
Why forgive an infant who has not chosen wrong?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / infant-question
The infant has chosen no wrong act, yet Augustine's church baptizes infants for forgiveness. This creates a question about what needs forgiving; the practice alone does not prove his answer.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / inherited-sin
Augustine: inherited sin, not an infant’s chosen act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / inherited-sin @ divinity-augustine-study
inherited sin, not an infant’s chosen act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / inherited-sin @ divinity-grace-alcove
inherited sin, not an infant’s chosen act

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / inherited-sin
Augustine says infants inherit a condition needing forgiveness through Adam, before choosing any wrong act themselves. Christians have disputed this teaching and its implications.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / condition-not-act
Augustine: both infants inherit sin; illness does not decide it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / condition-not-act @ divinity-augustine-study
both infants inherit sin; illness does not decide it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / condition-not-act @ divinity-grace-alcove
both infants inherit sin; illness does not decide it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / condition-not-act
In Augustine's account, both the ill infant and the healthy infant inherit Adam's sinful condition. Neither has chosen a wrong act. Their health is not what determines their need for forgiveness.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / tree-line
Matthew: “A good tree cannot bring forth evil fruit”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / tree-line
The verse concerns trees and fruit in a warning about false prophets. The medieval writer extends it into an argument about the sources of good and evil.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / source-rule
Two Principles: no evil can come from a wholly good cause

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / source-rule
The Book of Two Principles claims that a wholly good source cannot produce evil. That rule is an extra premise in its argument, not something proved merely by noticing suffering.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / harmful-world
Case: the same world feeds people and destroys them

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / harmful-world
Imagine fertile land and a destructive storm. The case asks what a good creator could be responsible for. It is not offered as evidence that any one disaster punishes its victims.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / maker-knows
Two Principles: a maker knowingly chose this harmful world

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / maker-knows
The text presses a responsibility question: if the maker knew the result and made its conditions, why treat that maker as only an observer?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / watcher-only
An observer can foresee harm without making its cause

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / watcher-only
Foreknowledge alone need not cause an event. This is the kind of reply examined in the text. A creator’s additional role still needs attention.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / good-source-limit
The writer turns the tree saying into a rule about causes

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / good-source-limit
The writer reads the tree-and-fruit saying as a rule about the sources of good and evil. Accepting the verse does not require accepting that particular interpretation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-sources
Two Principles: good and evil must have opposed sources

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-sources
The writer of the Book of Two Principles sees both good and harm in the world. Because the writer holds that a wholly good source cannot cause evil, the argument requires opposed sources. This conflicts with Augustine's account of evil as damaged goodness.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / maker-not-watcher
A knowing maker is not merely a knowing spectator

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / maker-not-watcher
Knowing that harm will happen is different from making the conditions that cause it. A reply about a passive observer does not fully answer the question about a knowing creator.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / origins-dispute
Is evil damaged goodness, or does it have its own source?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / origins-dispute
Augustine says evil is damage within a good creation. The writer of the Book of Two Principles argues that a wholly good source cannot cause evil, so evil must have an opposed source. The two accounts give different answers to the same problem of suffering and wrongdoing.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / fire-active
Aquinas: fire does what fire does—it heats and burns

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / fire-active
For Aquinas, fire functions well as fire when it heats and burns. That does not make it morally good or mean that every effect is good for everything nearby.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / water-lost
Aquinas’s example: the fire works, but destroys the water

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / water-lost
Aquinas imagines fire working according to its nature while destroying water. His medieval account concerns the loss of what makes it water, not a modern chemistry explanation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / reason-effects
Aquinas: the world can give reasons to believe in its cause

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / reason-effects
Aquinas thinks reasoning from effects can establish some things about their cause. “Creatures” in his text means created things, not only animals. He does not think such reasoning establishes every Christian belief.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / faith-beyond
Aquinas: the world cannot prove Father, Son and Spirit are one God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / faith-beyond
The Trinity is the belief that Father, Son and Holy Spirit are one God, not three gods. Aquinas says observation of created things cannot prove this belief.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / good-can-harm
Fire can work well as fire while harming something else

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / good-can-harm
Fire can function well as fire while harming something else. This challenges a rule that treats good functioning and harmless effects as the same thing.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-not-forced
Aquinas: harmful effects do not require a separate evil source

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-not-forced
Aquinas's example offers a way for harmful effects to come from something functioning well. It challenges the need for a separate evil source without answering every question about suffering.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / reason-boundary
Explaining a belief is not the same as proving it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / reason-boundary
Aquinas distinguishes explaining a belief from proving it. He allows reasoning about the Trinity while denying that observations of created things can establish it.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / limited-answer
The fire example challenges two sources, but leaves the maker’s responsibility open

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / limited-answer
The fire example challenges the claim that every harmful effect needs a wholly evil source. It still leaves the question of why a knowing creator would make a world in which such harm occurs.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / teacher-pupil
Teacher and pupil have different roles; both are human

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / teacher-pupil
A teacher and pupil have different roles while both remaining human. A difference in relationship does not automatically mean a difference in the kind of being they are.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / father-word
Augustine: Father and Son name a relationship, not two kinds of God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / father-word @ divinity-augustine-study
Father and Son name a relationship, not two kinds of God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / father-word @ divinity-grace-alcove
Father and Son name a relationship, not two kinds of God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / father-word
Being someone's father describes a relationship; it does not make the father a different kind of being from the child. Augustine uses this distinction to explain the names Father and Son while holding that both are divine.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / less-divine-question
Does being Son mean being a lesser kind of God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / less-divine-question
This is a question about an inference from the titles. Being related differently does not, just by itself, decide whether two are unequal in nature.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / three-humans
Case: three humans share a kind, but remain three beings

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / three-humans
Calling three people “human” does not turn them into a single human being. This comparison exposes a tempting but misleading way to explain divine unity.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / one-divine-being
Augustine: Father, Son and Spirit are one divine being

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / one-divine-being @ divinity-augustine-study
Father, Son and Spirit are one divine being

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / one-divine-being @ divinity-grace-alcove
Father, Son and Spirit are one divine being

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / one-divine-being
Augustine says Father, Son and Holy Spirit are one divine being. He also distinguishes them: the names do not all refer to the same relationship.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / son-addresses
Jesus: “Father … glorify thy Son”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / son-addresses
The Son addresses the Father. Augustine must explain how this real relationship belongs within his belief in one God.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / relation-not-kind
Different relationships need not mean different kinds of being

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / relation-not-kind
Teacher and pupil have different roles while both being human. Augustine similarly treats Father and Son as names of a relationship, not names for greater and lesser kinds of God.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / not-less-divine
Augustine: the name Son does not mean a lesser God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-less-divine @ divinity-augustine-study
the name Son does not mean a lesser God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-less-divine @ divinity-grace-alcove
the name Son does not mean a lesser God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / not-less-divine
Augustine says Son names a relationship to the Father, not an inferior kind of God. On his account, Father and Son share the same divine nature.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / not-three-members
Augustine: one God—not three gods sharing a kind

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-three-members @ divinity-augustine-study
one God—not three gods sharing a kind

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-three-members @ divinity-grace-alcove
one God—not three gods sharing a kind

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / not-three-members
Three humans share a kind but remain three separate beings. Augustine's claim is different: Father, Son and Spirit are one divine being with real distinctions, not three similar gods.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / not-masks
Augustine: Father and Son are distinct, not one actor changing masks

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-masks @ divinity-augustine-study
Father and Son are distinct, not one actor changing masks

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / not-masks @ divinity-grace-alcove
Father and Son are distinct, not one actor changing masks

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / not-masks
In Augustine's account, Father and Son are genuinely distinct: the Son addresses the Father. They are not one actor successively wearing different costumes.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trinity-bounded
Trinity: one God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trinity-bounded
The doctrine of the Trinity names Father, Son and Holy Spirit as one God. They are not separate gods or one person taking turns in different roles. Aquinas says observation of created things cannot prove this doctrine.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / book-and-hands
Lyon ritual: a Gospel book and hands touch the candidate

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / book-and-hands
A Gospel is an account of Jesus’s life and teaching. The ritual places the book and hands on the candidate. These are physical actions, even though the ceremony claims an inward gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / spirit-baptism
Lyon ritual: laying on hands gives forgiveness and God’s Spirit

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / spirit-baptism
The Lyon rite promises forgiveness and the Holy Spirit, understood as God dwelling with the believer. It calls the laying on of hands spiritual baptism rather than washing with water.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / acts-hands-line
Acts: Jesus’s first followers lay on hands to give the Spirit

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / acts-hands-line
Acts is the biblical account of the early Christian community. The ritual cites its report that Peter and John lay hands on believers who receive the Holy Spirit. The appeal claims authority; it is not independent verification of this later rite.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / world-renunciation
Lyon candidate promises to turn from worldly desires

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / world-renunciation
The candidate promises to turn away from “worldly desires”. This concerns how the person should live; it does not state a complete explanation of who created matter.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / enemy-love
Jesus: “Love your enemies”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / enemy-love
Loving enemies is one of the candidate's obligations. Turning away from worldly desires therefore cannot mean abandoning concern for other people.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / apostolic-claim
Lyon ritual claims to continue the first followers’ laying on of hands

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / apostolic-claim
The Lyon ritual appeals to the laying on of hands described among Jesus's first followers. Its ministers claim authority by continuing that practice.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / bodily-spiritual
The rite claims a spiritual gift through hands and a book

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / bodily-spiritual
The ritual uses a person's body, ministers' hands and a Gospel book while claiming a spiritual gift. Physical action and a claimed inward effect are not simple opposites.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / ritual-not-cosmology
Using bodies in a rite does not prove belief in one good creator

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / ritual-not-cosmology
In the Lyon ritual, ministers lay hands and a Gospel book on a believer while promising forgiveness and God’s Spirit. Using physical objects does not tell us whether this community believed in one good creator or opposed sources of good and evil. The Book of Two Principles argues for opposed sources; the ritual does not state that account.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / renounce-not-abandon
Rejecting worldly desires does not cancel the duty to love enemies

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / renounce-not-abandon
The candidate promises to reject worldly desires and to love enemies. Giving up those desires does not remove duties toward other people.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / axe-cuts
Aquinas’s example: the axe cuts the wood

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / axe-cuts
The edge explains the tool’s ordinary cutting. It does not explain by itself why a particular piece of furniture takes shape.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / craftsman-shapes
The craftsperson uses the axe to make a bed

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / craftsman-shapes
The craftsperson and the tool act in different, connected ways. Calling the axe effective does not install a second independent designer.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / water-washes
Baptism uses water to wash the body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / water-washes
Water has an ordinary bodily action. Aquinas cites Augustine’s distinction between touching the body and cleansing the heart.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / god-grace
Aquinas: only God gives grace—new life within the soul

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / god-grace
For Aquinas, grace is God’s gift of a new share in divine life, changing the soul from within. God alone is its ultimate source.

His question is whether a bodily rite can truly help bring about that gift without becoming a second, independent source of divine power.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / instituted-baptism
Aquinas: God appointed baptism with water to give grace—new life within the soul

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / instituted-baptism
Aquinas says God chose baptism as a way to give grace: a new life within the soul. That is why its water has a role beyond ordinary washing.

A sacrament is such a God-appointed bodily sign or rite. The axe comparison cannot prove that God appointed any particular rite.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / nature-perfected-line
Aquinas: “grace does not destroy nature, but perfects it”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / nature-perfected-line
God's gift of help develops human powers such as reasoning and willing rather than erasing them. Aquinas describes reason serving faith and willing guided by love. This does not mean a person becomes incapable of error, and questions about free choice remain.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / dependent-tool
The axe cuts; the craftsperson works through it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / dependent-tool
The axe cuts the wood, while the craftsperson makes the bed through it. Both act, but the axe is not a second independent maker. Aquinas uses this pattern to explain how God could act through a rite.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / dependent-grace
Aquinas: a rite can be God’s tool, not a second god

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / dependent-grace
Aquinas argues that God can act through a rite as a craftsperson acts through a tool. This explains how the rite need not rival God, but does not prove that God appointed any particular ceremony.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / baptism-appointed
Aquinas: God chose baptism with water to give inward grace, not just wash the body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / baptism-appointed
Aquinas believes God chose baptism with water to give inward grace. The water acts through God's choice; it does not gain independent divine power.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / instrumental-baptism
Aquinas: in baptism, God works through water to give inward grace

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / instrumental-baptism
For Aquinas, baptism washes the body while God works through the water to give grace: a new life within the soul. The water is a tool, not a second god. This depends on his belief that God chose baptism to give that gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / help-not-erasure
God’s help need not stop a person from really choosing

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / help-not-erasure
Augustine's helped person still chooses, and Aquinas's tool still acts. The comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not mean people are merely tools.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / rites-different
Both rites use bodies; they disagree about what God appointed

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / rites-different
The Lyon rite uses hands and a Gospel book; Aquinas discusses baptism with water. Both claim an inward gift through physical actions, but disagree about the authorized rite and how it gives that gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / bread-taste
Case: after the church’s blessing, it still looks and tastes like bread

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / bread-taste
After the blessing, the bread looks and tastes the same. Aquinas's account must explain why he still says that what it is has changed.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / supper-words
Jesus: “This is my body”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / supper-words
Christians dispute what these supper words mean. Aquinas reads them as affirming the real presence of Christ’s body, not only a reminder.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / aquinas-change
Aquinas: what was bread becomes Christ’s body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / aquinas-change
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ’s body, although its taste, colour and shape remain. He is not saying that it starts to look or taste like flesh.

This claim is called transubstantiation: a change in what the thing is, while its appearances remain. Other Christians dispute the claim.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / appearance-rule
Aquinas: what a thing is can differ from how it looks and tastes

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / appearance-rule
Aquinas distinguishes what something is from its appearance. In Communion, he says the bread's substance—what it is—changes, while qualities such as taste and colour remain. He calls those remaining qualities accidents.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / changed-claim
Aquinas reads Christ’s words as changing bread into his body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / changed-claim
Aquinas reads Christ's supper words as saying that bread becomes Christ's body. This is a particular interpretation, not the only possible reading established by the words alone.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / same-appearances
For Aquinas, unchanged taste does not mean unchanged reality

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / same-appearances
Aquinas says the appearance stays the same while what the thing is changes. Unchanged taste therefore does not contradict his account, but it does not verify it either.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / eucharist-account
Aquinas: it becomes Christ’s body but keeps bread’s look and taste

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / eucharist-account
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ's body while keeping the look and taste of bread. This is called transubstantiation. Explaining the claim about Communion does not establish that the change occurred.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / not-sensory-proof
This explains Aquinas’s claim; tasting the bread does not prove it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / not-sensory-proof
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ’s body while its taste remains. Tasting it therefore cannot prove the change he claims. His account rests on faith in divine authority, not a change detected by the senses.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-material-roles
Aquinas: baptism’s water is God’s tool; Communion’s bread becomes Christ’s body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-material-roles
For Aquinas, baptism's water is a tool through which God acts. In Communion, what was bread becomes Christ's body while appearances remain. These give physical things different roles.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / single-coin
Case: the household has one coin and no supper

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / single-coin
The household has one coin and no supper. Spending it on an indulgence would leave the family's immediate need for food unmet.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / seller-guarantee
Seller’s claim reported by Luther: a coin frees a dead soul

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / seller-guarantee
Luther reports sellers promising that payment releases a dead person from punishment. He attacks the claim that a coin can guarantee that result.

The punishment in question is purgatory: purification after death for people ultimately saved, not hell. A reported sales pitch is not the same as official doctrine.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / letter-penalties
An indulgence concerns penalties remaining after sins are forgiven

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / letter-penalties
In this debate, being forgiven can still leave penalties to be dealt with. An indulgence concerns those penalties; it is not supposed to buy forgiveness itself.

Luther questions what the pope can release. The seller’s promised release of a dead soul is a disputed sales claim, not an official definition of Catholic teaching.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / care-first-rule
Luther: household necessities and poor neighbours come first

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / care-first-rule
Luther gives care a positive priority, rather than treating the purchase as the only religious use for money.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / declare-forgiveness
Luther: the pope announces forgiveness; God is the one who forgives

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / declare-forgiveness
In 1517 Luther still allows a ministerial role. Declaring God’s forgiveness is not an independent power to make God forgive on receipt of money.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / repent-whole-life
Luther: repentance means a lifetime of turning away from wrong

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / repent-whole-life
Repentance is turning from wrong, not a transaction completed once and forgotten. This claim helps explain why a guarantee based on payment troubles Luther.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / coin-not-control
For Luther, a seller cannot guarantee God’s mercy for a coin

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / coin-not-control
Luther rejects a seller's guarantee that a payment will release a dead soul. A limited church role does not give the seller control over God's mercy.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / feed-before-letter
Luther: buy the hungry household supper before an indulgence letter

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / feed-before-letter
Luther says a household with one coin and no supper should buy food before an indulgence letter—a grant of relief from penalties for sins already forgiven. Feeding the family and helping poor neighbours come first.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / ministry-not-sale
Working for God does not mean controlling God’s gifts

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / ministry-not-sale
Luther says church ministers can announce God’s forgiveness but cannot make God forgive for money. Aquinas says baptism’s water acts through God’s power, not its own. Both distinguish serving God from independently controlling God’s gifts, although they address different practices.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / no-paid-shortcut
Luther: a paid guarantee cannot replace a changed life

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / no-paid-shortcut
For Luther, a guarantee based on payment cannot replace repentance and a changed life. This criticism is not a blanket claim that every church rite or payment is fraudulent.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / gift-acceptance
Luther: faith (trust in Christ), not good deeds, makes us right with God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / gift-acceptance
Luther says faith, or trust in Christ and his promise, makes a person right with God. This is called justification. Helping a neighbour meets a real need, but does not earn God's acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / why-help
Case: if God accepts me without payment, why feed anyone?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / why-help
A reader takes Luther's rejection of earning God's acceptance as a reason to do nothing. But a neighbour still needs help. What should good deeds accomplish?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / neighbour-still-hungry
Case: the neighbour’s hunger has not vanished

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / neighbour-still-hungry
Freedom from buying God’s acceptance does not itself supply the neighbour’s food. The case asks who a good work is for.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / christ-serves
Luther: Christ served me freely; I can freely serve my neighbour

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / christ-serves
Luther says Christians should serve their neighbours freely, as Christ served them. They help because the neighbour needs it, not to earn God's acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / body-practice
Luther: train the body to serve, not to earn God’s acceptance

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / body-practice
The text does not discard bodily life. Work and discipline remain, but their role must be distinguished from earning acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / no-wages
Not earning God’s acceptance does not make helping others pointless

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / no-wages
Feeding someone can help them even if it does not earn acceptance by God. Luther gives service a purpose in the neighbour's need rather than treating it as a payment for salvation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / free-service
Luther: feed the neighbour because they need food, not to earn salvation

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / free-service
Luther calls for service because the neighbour needs help, following Christ's freely given service. The deed is not a price paid to earn God's acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / care-not-purchase
Feeding the household helps people; it does not buy acceptance by God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / care-not-purchase
The household still needs supper. For Luther, feeding them serves that need without buying acceptance by God. A good deed can matter to other people without becoming payment for salvation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-grace-questions
Augustine: what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / two-grace-questions @ divinity-augustine-study
what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / owned-location tile/reader-caption / two-grace-questions @ divinity-grace-alcove
what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-grace-questions
Augustine asks how God helps a person want and do what is right. Luther asks what makes a person right with God. Both concern God's gift, but answering one question does not fully answer the other.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / practice-not-price
Luther: bodily discipline trains me; it does not buy God’s acceptance

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / practice-not-price
For Luther, bodily work and discipline can train a person for service. Effort matters without becoming the price of God's acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / remembered-ring
Zwingli: a ring recalls a beloved without containing their body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / remembered-ring
A ring can bring a loved person to mind without containing their body. Zwingli uses this example to explain how Communion's bread can point to Christ without containing his body.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / body-in-heaven
Zwingli: Christ’s human body is in heaven

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / body-in-heaven
Zwingli argues that Christ's human body is in heaven after the Ascension. He therefore reads the Communion bread as a sign of that body, not a place where the body is located.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / luther-word-makes
Luther: Christ’s words give his body with the bread

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / luther-word-makes
In the 1529 catechism, the sacrament depends on Christ’s words, not the participant’s ability to create it by believing. This is not Aquinas’s account of changed bread.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / unbelieving-guest
Case: a Communion guest eats the bread without trusting Christ’s promise

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / unbelieving-guest
The guest tests two questions: what is received, and whether receiving it brings the promised benefit. The two need not have the same answer in Luther’s account.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / faith-benefit
Luther: Communion’s promised forgiveness is received only through faith—trust in Christ’s promise

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / faith-benefit
For Luther, the promised forgiveness in Communion is received through faith: trusting Christ's promise that the gift is for you. Merely eating does not supply that trust. He distinguishes receiving Christ's body from receiving the forgiveness that also strengthens faith.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / signed-trinity
Marburg’s signers affirm one God in three persons

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / signed-trinity
The jointly signed first article affirms the Trinity. Agreement on this matter does not remove their dispute about the supper.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / signed-supper
Marburg: Christ is received through faith; his bodily presence stays disputed

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / signed-supper
The signers affirm receiving Christ through faith and the supper's strengthening of faith. They still disagree about Christ's bodily presence in bread and wine; they do not share one account of how that presence works.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / zwingli-reading
Zwingli: bread points to Christ’s body; it does not contain it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / zwingli-reading
Zwingli's ring recalls someone without containing their body. Together with his belief that Christ's human body is in heaven, this supports his reading of Communion bread as a sign. Aquinas and Luther give different accounts of the same supper words.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-presence-accounts
Aquinas: bread becomes Christ’s body. Luther: Christ’s body comes with bread

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-presence-accounts
Both affirm bodily presence, but the material accounts differ. Aquinas describes a change in what was bread; Luther’s catechism calls it bread with Christ’s body.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / three-supper-accounts
Aquinas: bread becomes the body. Luther: body with bread. Zwingli: bread signifies body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / three-supper-accounts
Aquinas: the bread becomes Christ’s body while appearances remain. Luther: Christ’s body is given with the bread. Zwingli: the bread signifies the body, which is in heaven.

These are three different accounts of Communion. Shared words about Christ do not make their claims about bread and body identical.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / guest-not-maker
Luther: even an unbelieving Communion guest receives Christ’s body with the bread

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / guest-not-maker
In Luther’s account of Communion, even the guest who does not believe receives Christ’s body with the bread. Christ’s words establish what is given; the guest’s disbelief does not undo it.

This answers what is received. Whether receiving it brings the promised forgiveness is a separate question.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / received-not-benefited
Luther: the unbelieving Communion guest receives Christ’s body, not the promised forgiveness

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / received-not-benefited
Luther says an unbelieving Communion guest still receives Christ's body with the bread, because Christ's words establish the gift. Without trusting the promise, the guest does not receive its promised forgiveness. What is given and its benefit to the receiver are separate questions.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / shared-trinity
They agree about one God in three persons—not every claim about Communion

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / shared-trinity
The writers can share belief in one God as Father, Son and Spirit while disagreeing about Communion. Real agreement does not require agreement on every question.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / bounded-agreement
Shared belief in the Trinity leaves their Communion dispute open

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / bounded-agreement
At Marburg, the signers could affirm one God in three persons and receiving Christ through faith. They still disagreed about Christ’s body being present in the bread and wine.

A shared signature does not erase the disagreement. The 1529 articles record both the agreement and its limit.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-first-help
Council of Trent: God helps first, before an adult responds

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-first-help
Trent begins with God's help, which the person has not earned. Its account does not say that people start by saving themselves.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-can-refuse
Council of Trent: an adult can accept God’s help or refuse it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-can-refuse
Trent says an adult can accept or refuse help that God gives first. The person's response is real, while the first gift remains unearned.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-no-price
Council of Trent: being made right with God for the first time is not earned by faith or deeds

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-no-price
The Council of Trent says that nothing before our first being made right with God—neither faith nor deeds—earns that gift. It begins with God’s help.

This is initial justification. Keeping that limit visible prevents the false contrast “Luther teaches a gift; Trent teaches buying it”.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-growth
Trent council: later deeds, helped by God, deepen received righteousness—our renewed inner life with God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-growth
For Trent, righteousness includes God renewing the person within, not merely a change in reputation. After the initial gift, good deeds made possible by God’s help increase this received righteousness.

This is the council’s account of growth in justification. It is not just the observation that repeated practice can improve someone’s outward behaviour, and it does not say people begin by earning salvation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / luther-fruit
Luther: deeds can train us, but do not make a Christian more holy before God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / luther-fruit
In Christian Liberty, Luther gives deeds a role in training the body and serving others, but denies that those deeds make an already-Christian person more holy before God.

His bishop comparison makes the distinction: doing the bishop’s work does not make the bishop more consecrated. The text allows growth of faith and love, so this is not a denial of all growth or better conduct.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / assisted-response
Trent: God helps us respond; we do not start by saving ourselves

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / assisted-response
In Trent's account, God's help comes first and a person really responds. Receiving help does not mean there is no human action.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / assisted-consent
Trent: God helps first, but the adult really accepts or refuses

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / assisted-consent
Trent says the adult can accept or refuse God's prior help. Having a real choice does not mean the response earns the gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / response-not-price
Trent council: an adult’s response does not earn being made right with God for the first time

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / response-not-price
Trent describes an adult responding to God's help without earning the initial gift of being made right with God. This addresses initial justification, not every later role of good deeds.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / gift-common-ground
Luther and Trent: feeding someone does not earn our first acceptance by God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / gift-common-ground
In this household case, both accounts deny that feeding someone buys the initial gift of being made right with God. That limited agreement does not make their accounts of faith, inner renewal and later deeds identical.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / works-dispute
Trent and Luther dispute how deeds affect holiness before God—not whether conduct can improve

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / works-dispute
Trent says deeds made possible by God's help increase the righteousness already received, including inward renewal. Luther's text denies that deeds make an already-Christian person more holy before God, while valuing training and service. The disagreement is about the role of deeds before God, not merely whether behaviour improves.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / friend-request
Case: “Please pray for my child,” to a living friend

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / friend-request
The request asks another person to pray to God. It does not ask the friend to replace God or to supply divine power.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / calvin-mutual
Calvin allows living believers to pray for one another

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / calvin-mutual
Calvin allows Christians to ask a living neighbour to pray. He does not think this replaces Christ's unique role between people and God.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / saint-request
Case: to a dead saint, “Stephen, pray for my child”

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / saint-request
Someone asks a departed saint to pray to God on their behalf. They are asking for prayer, not a second saviour. Calvin and Trent disagree about whether people should make this request.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / calvin-hearing
Calvin: asking dead saints for prayer lacks biblical permission and obscures Christ’s unique role before God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / calvin-hearing
Calvin asks what biblical authority permits addressing saints who have died, and how they would know particular requests. He also thinks the practice draws attention away from Christ’s unique work for us.

That work is called mediation: Christ’s role between people and God. Saying that the saint is only a helper does not answer Calvin’s objections.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / stephen-panel
Case: a painting of Stephen, proposed for prayerful honour in a chapel

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / stephen-panel
The proposed chapel painting shows Stephen remaining faithful. The question is whether people should direct religious honour through it, not whether all paintings should be forbidden.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / calvin-pictures
Calvin allows some pictures, but not honouring them in worship

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / calvin-pictures
Calvin is not simply against all art. His distinction concerns the use made of pictures in worship, including whether honour can safely be directed through them.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / stephen-fragment
Case: a bone labelled “Stephen”; nobody has supplied its history

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / stephen-fragment
The bone is labelled Stephen, but there is no record of where it was found or who kept it. The label alone does not establish whose bone it is.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / label-not-proof
A label records a claim; it does not prove identity

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / label-not-proof
A name on a label states whose bone someone claims this is. It does not prove the identity. Even a tradition that permits relics still needs evidence about this particular object.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / calvin-even-genuine
Calvin: even a real saint’s bone should not receive religious honour

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / calvin-even-genuine
Calvin objects both to doubtful relic claims and to giving relics religious honour. Establishing a bone's identity would not answer his objection to the practice.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / living-prayer
Calvin: asking a living friend to pray does not replace Christ

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / living-prayer
Calvin permits asking a living friend to pray while relying on Christ. This answers the living-friend case, not every proposed request for help.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / saint-warrant-question
Calvin: asking dead saints for prayer lacks biblical permission and obscures Christ’s unique work of speaking for us before God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / saint-warrant-question
Calvin argues that Scripture does not authorize asking departed saints for prayer and that doing so obscures Christ's unique work for people before God. Calling the saint a helper does not answer these objections.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / agency-not-permission
God can use helpers; Calvin still says asking dead saints for prayer lacks biblical permission and sidelines Christ

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / agency-not-permission
Aquinas says God can work through baptism’s water. But water is not a person being asked to pray, so this example does not decide whether people should address saints who have died. Calvin rejects those requests: he says they lack biblical permission and obscure Christ’s unique work of bringing people before God.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / calvin-panel-answer
Calvin rejects honouring this painting in worship, not all paintings

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / calvin-panel-answer
Calvin rejects giving this painting a role in religious honour. His objection concerns its use in worship, not the existence of every painted picture.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / fragment-unverified
The name on the bone does not establish whose bone it is

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / fragment-unverified
The fragment's label is not enough to establish whose bone it is. The honest result is uncertainty, not an authenticated relic.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-saints-pray
Trent: saints with Christ pray for people on earth

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-saints-pray
Trent teaches that saints with Christ continue to pray for people on Earth. The council therefore recommends asking for their help.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-christ-alone
Trent: Christ alone saves; saints are not other saviours

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-christ-alone
The council does not present saints as competing saviours. It nevertheless disagrees with Calvin about the legitimacy of requesting their aid.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-image-honour
Trent: honour the person shown in the picture, not the paint

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-image-honour
The council denies that divinity or power resides in an image as such. It gives images a role in remembrance, instruction and honour directed to their subjects.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-body-honour
Trent: saints’ bodies deserve honour because they belong to Christ

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-body-honour
The decree treats holy bodies as connected with Christ and with resurrection. This is a reason within its teaching, not an automatic identification of any labelled fragment.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / trent-new-relic
Trent: newly claimed relics need examination and a bishop’s approval

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / trent-new-relic
The decree restricts recognition of new relics and approval of unusual images. A permissible category is not a licence for every particular object.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / new-record
Case update: a record of who has kept this bone

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / new-record
A new record identifies some of the people who kept the bone. It strengthens the investigation, but gaps in its history remain and a bishop has not recognized it as a relic.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / saints-not-saviours
Trent: ask saints to pray through Christ, not to replace him

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / saints-not-saviours
Trent says saints with Christ pray for people on earth. Asking for their prayers therefore means asking for help through Christ, not replacing him. Calvin still rejects the practice because he finds no biblical permission for it and thinks it obscures Christ's unique role.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / prayer-disagreement
Trent: saints pray through Christ. Calvin: no biblical permission; Christ is sidelined

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / prayer-disagreement
Trent says saints pray through Christ rather than replacing him. Calvin rejects asking them, arguing that it lacks biblical permission and obscures Christ's unique role. They disagree about whether people should make these requests, not simply about what to call the saints.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / panel-possible
Trent allows honour directed through the painting to Stephen

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / panel-possible
Trent allows honour to pass through a painting to the person shown. The proposed Stephen painting fits that kind of use; the paint itself is not treated as divine.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / panel-still-disputed
Trent permits this honour through a picture; Calvin rejects it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / panel-still-disputed
Trent says honour can pass through the picture to Stephen. Calvin rejects that devotional use. Both have now considered the same proposed painting.

Redirecting the honour away from the paint explains Trent’s position; it does not make Calvin accept it.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / honour-needs-identity
Honouring saints’ bones requires knowing this bone is a saint’s

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / honour-needs-identity
A reason to honour genuine relics does not establish that this fragment is genuine. The object's identity still needs evidence.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / recognition-pending
This bone still needs examination and a bishop’s recognition

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / recognition-pending
The claim about this bone still needs examination and a bishop's recognition. A name on its label and a partial history are not enough.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / record-not-ruling
A record of the bone’s past helps; it is not a bishop’s approval

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / record-not-ruling
The new record helps trace who kept the bone. It does not verify every link in its history or amount to a bishop's approval.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / evidence-not-consensus
A better history for the bone would not remove Calvin’s objection

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / evidence-not-consensus
A better history could support the bone's claimed identity, but Calvin would still reject religious honour to it. Whose bone it is and whether it should be venerated are different questions.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / two-infants
Case: one infant ill, one well; neither chose wrong

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / two-infants
One infant is ill and the other is well. Neither has chosen a wrongful act. Does either child's health determine a need for forgiveness?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / health-not-moral-test
The sick infant has not chosen wrong; neither has the healthy infant

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / health-not-moral-test
Neither infant has chosen a wrong act. Illness in one child is not evidence that the child deliberately did wrong.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / papal-penalty-limit
Luther: the pope can lift church penalties, not control God’s mercy

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / papal-penalty-limit
In 1517 Luther limits the pope's own power to penalties imposed by church authority. He distinguishes declaring God's forgiveness from controlling it. The limits he proposes are disputed.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / letter-limited
Luther: an indulgence letter cannot command God to free a dead soul

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / letter-limited
An indulgence concerns penalties remaining after forgiveness. Luther further limits church authority: a papal letter cannot by its own power command God to release a dead soul.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / luther-zwingli-contrast
Luther: Christ’s body comes with bread. Zwingli: bread points to the body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / luther-zwingli-contrast
These two accounts differ about bodily presence. That does not yet locate Aquinas: his earlier changed-bread account is a third position to compare.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / authenticity-not-permission
Even if it is Stephen’s bone, Calvin still rejects honouring it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / authenticity-not-permission
The bone's identity remains unresolved, but Calvin's objection to honouring it does not depend on that uncertainty. A genuine object can still be disputed as a religious practice.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / tile/reader-caption / record-adds-evidence
The record helps trace the bone; it does not yet prove its identity

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / reader-body / record-adds-evidence
The record adds evidence about the fragment's past. Its incomplete history does not yet establish the bone's identity or supply a bishop's approval.

