## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / objective / axe-and-water
How could water give an inward gift?
Are these two rites saying the same thing?
Can we really act when God helps us?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / entry fallback / axe-and-water
The next morning, the desk is ready for a new comparison. Aquinas compares a religious rite with a tool through which someone acts. Use the axe example to clarify his account of baptism, while keeping separate his belief that God appointed this particular rite.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / completion/ending / axe-and-water
For Aquinas, God can act through baptism as a craftsperson acts through a tool. He also holds that God appointed this rite; ordinary water is not automatically a sacrament.

