## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-trinity-bounded
The doctrine of the Trinity names Father, Son and Holy Spirit as one God. They are not separate gods or one person taking turns in different roles. Aquinas says observation of created things cannot prove this doctrine.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-trinity-bounded
Trinity: one God as Father, Son and Holy Spirit

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-ritual-not-cosmology
In the Lyon ritual, ministers lay hands and a Gospel book on a believer while promising forgiveness and God’s Spirit. Using physical objects does not tell us whether this community believed in one good creator or opposed sources of good and evil. The Book of Two Principles argues for opposed sources; the ritual does not state that account.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-ritual-not-cosmology
Using bodies in a rite does not prove belief in one good creator

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-renounce-not-abandon
The candidate promises to reject worldly desires and to love enemies. Giving up those desires does not remove duties toward other people.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-renounce-not-abandon
Rejecting worldly desires does not cancel the duty to love enemies

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-instrumental-baptism
For Aquinas, baptism washes the body while God works through the water to give grace: a new life within the soul. The water is a tool, not a second god. This depends on his belief that God chose baptism to give that gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-instrumental-baptism
Aquinas: in baptism, God works through water to give inward grace

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-rites-different
The Lyon rite uses hands and a Gospel book; Aquinas discusses baptism with water. Both claim an inward gift through physical actions, but disagree about the authorized rite and how it gives that gift.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-rites-different
Both rites use bodies; they disagree about what God appointed

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-help-not-erasure
Augustine says a person still chooses when helped by God. Aquinas says an axe really cuts while a craftsperson works through it. The limited comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not reduce people to tools.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-help-not-erasure
God’s help need not stop a person from really choosing

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-not-sensory-proof
Aquinas says the bread becomes Christ’s body while its taste remains. Tasting it therefore cannot prove the change he claims. His account rests on faith in divine authority, not a change detected by the senses.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-not-sensory-proof
This explains Aquinas’s claim; tasting the bread does not prove it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-two-material-roles
For Aquinas, baptism's water is a tool through which God acts. In Communion, what was bread becomes Christ's body while appearances remain. These give physical things different roles.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-two-material-roles
Aquinas: baptism’s water is God’s tool; Communion’s bread becomes Christ’s body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-feed-before-letter
Luther says a household with one coin and no supper should buy food before an indulgence letter—a grant of relief from penalties for sins already forgiven. Feeding the family and helping poor neighbours come first.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-feed-before-letter
Luther: buy the hungry household supper before an indulgence letter

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-ministry-not-sale
Luther says church ministers can announce God’s forgiveness but cannot make God forgive for money. Aquinas says baptism’s water acts through God’s power, not its own. Both distinguish serving God from independently controlling God’s gifts, although they address different practices.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-ministry-not-sale
Working for God does not mean controlling God’s gifts

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-no-paid-shortcut
Luther describes repentance as a lifetime of turning away from wrong. A seller's promise that a coin guarantees release for a dead soul cannot replace that changed life or give the seller control over God's mercy. His criticism does not declare every church rite or payment fraudulent.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-no-paid-shortcut
Luther: a paid guarantee cannot replace a changed life

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-care-not-purchase
The household still needs supper. For Luther, feeding them serves that need without buying acceptance by God. A good deed can matter to other people without becoming payment for salvation.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-care-not-purchase
Feeding the household helps people; it does not buy acceptance by God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-two-grace-questions
Augustine asks how God helps a person want and do what is right. Luther asks what makes a person right with God. Both concern God's gift, but answering one question does not fully answer the other.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-two-grace-questions
Augustine: what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder owned-location italic caption / plain-divinity-two-grace-questions @ divinity-augustine-study
what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder owned-location italic caption / plain-divinity-two-grace-questions @ divinity-grace-alcove
what helps me act? Luther: what makes me right with God?

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-practice-not-price
For Luther, bodily work and discipline can train a person for service. Effort matters without becoming the price of God's acceptance.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-practice-not-price
Luther: bodily discipline trains me; it does not buy God’s acceptance

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-three-supper-accounts
Aquinas: the bread becomes Christ’s body while appearances remain. Luther: Christ’s body is given with the bread. Zwingli: the bread signifies the body, which is in heaven.

These are three different accounts of Communion. Shared words about Christ do not make their claims about bread and body identical.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-three-supper-accounts
Aquinas: bread becomes the body. Luther: body with bread. Zwingli: bread signifies body

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-received-not-benefited
Luther says an unbelieving Communion guest still receives Christ's body with the bread, because Christ's words establish the gift. Without trusting the promise, the guest does not receive its promised forgiveness. What is given and its benefit to the receiver are separate questions.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-received-not-benefited
Luther: the unbelieving Communion guest receives Christ’s body, not the promised forgiveness

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-bounded-agreement
At Marburg, the signers affirm one God as Father, Son and Spirit and receiving Christ through faith. They still disagree about his bodily presence in bread and wine. A shared signature records real agreement without erasing its limit.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-bounded-agreement
Shared belief in the Trinity leaves their Communion dispute open

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-response-not-price
Trent describes an adult responding to God's help without earning the initial gift of being made right with God. This addresses initial justification, not every later role of good deeds.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-response-not-price
Trent council: an adult’s response does not earn being made right with God for the first time

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-gift-common-ground
Luther and the Council of Trent both deny, in this household comparison, that feeding someone buys the initial gift of being made right with God. This limited agreement does not make their accounts of faith, inward renewal or later good deeds the same.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-gift-common-ground
Luther and Trent: feeding someone does not earn our first acceptance by God

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-works-dispute
Trent says deeds made possible by God's help increase the righteousness already received, including inward renewal. Luther's text denies that deeds make an already-Christian person more holy before God, while valuing training and service. The disagreement is about the role of deeds before God, not merely whether behaviour improves.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-works-dispute
Trent and Luther dispute how deeds affect holiness before God—not whether conduct can improve

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-living-prayer
Calvin permits asking a living friend to pray while relying on Christ. This answers the living-friend case, not every proposed request for help.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-living-prayer
Calvin: asking a living friend to pray does not replace Christ

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-agency-not-permission
Aquinas says God can work through baptism’s water. But water is not a person being asked to pray, so this example does not decide whether people should address saints who have died. Calvin rejects those requests: he says they lack biblical permission and obscure Christ’s unique work of bringing people before God.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-agency-not-permission
God can use helpers; Calvin still says asking dead saints for prayer lacks biblical permission and sidelines Christ

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-calvin-panel-answer
Calvin rejects giving this painting a role in religious honour. His objection concerns its use in worship, not the existence of every painted picture.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-calvin-panel-answer
Calvin rejects honouring this painting in worship, not all paintings

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-fragment-unverified
The fragment's label is not enough to establish whose bone it is. The honest result is uncertainty, not an authenticated relic.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-fragment-unverified
The name on the bone does not establish whose bone it is

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-prayer-disagreement
Trent says saints pray through Christ rather than replacing him. Calvin rejects asking them, arguing that it lacks biblical permission and obscures Christ's unique role. They disagree about whether people should make these requests, not simply about what to call the saints.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-prayer-disagreement
Trent: saints pray through Christ. Calvin: no biblical permission; Christ is sidelined

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-panel-still-disputed
Trent says honour can pass through the picture to Stephen. Calvin rejects that devotional use. Both have now considered the same proposed painting.

Redirecting the honour away from the paint explains Trent’s position; it does not make Calvin accept it.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-panel-still-disputed
Trent permits this honour through a picture; Calvin rejects it

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder body / plain-divinity-evidence-not-consensus
A new record may help establish whose bone the fragment is. Calvin would still reject giving it religious honour even if its identity were proved, while Trent allows honour to genuine relics. Better identity evidence does not settle that religious disagreement.

## divinity-final-reading-scenes-v7 / wonder italic caption / plain-divinity-evidence-not-consensus
A better history for the bone would not remove Calvin’s objection

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / chooser / divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4
Divinity-light · Who Is Still Outside?

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / chooser / divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4
Read a roadside story, a family reunion and two accounts of a feeding, then check what Luther says about helping people in need.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / law-answer
Jewish law expert: love your neighbour; Jesus agrees

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / law-answer
The lawyer answers with love of God and neighbor. Jesus tells him that he has answered correctly. Both speakers are Jewish; this is a discussion within Jewish scriptural teaching.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / first-question
Law expert to Jesus: Who is my neighbour?

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / first-question
The lawyer asks Jesus, “Who is my neighbor?” Jesus responds with a teaching story, a parable, about a traveler on the road. Watch what Jesus asks at the other end of the story.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / two-passers
A priest and a Levite pass by

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / two-passers
In the story, an injured traveler is left beside the road. A priest sees him and passes by on the other side. A Levite also sees him and passes by. The story does not tell us their motives.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / samaritan-leaf
A Samaritan tends the traveller and pays for care

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / samaritan-leaf
A Samaritan sees the traveler and is moved with compassion. He tends his wounds, takes him to an inn, and pays for care. He also promises to cover further costs. Jesus makes this person the helper in his story.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / community-note
Jews and Samaritans: related communities, disputed worship

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / community-note
Jews and Samaritans shared connections to Israel's Scriptures but disagreed about matters including the proper place of worship. Jesus is Jewish, and the helper in his story is a Samaritan.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / last-question
Jesus: Which person acted as a neighbour?

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / last-question
Jesus asks which of the three seemed to be a neighbor to the injured man. The lawyer answers that it was the one who showed mercy. Jesus replies, “Go and do likewise.”

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / stranger-command
Leviticus: love the stranger as yourself

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / stranger-command
Leviticus says of the stranger living among the people: “you shall love him as yourself; for you lived as foreigners in the land of Egypt.” This instruction is in Jewish Scripture, long before the Gospel story.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / who-stopped
The priest and Levite pass; the Samaritan stops

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / who-stopped
The story contrasts passing by with stopping to give practical care. Its helper is a Samaritan. That is a contrast between these characters’ actions, not a ranking of their communities.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / crossed-boundary
The help crosses a community boundary

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / crossed-boundary
The traveler receives care from someone across a familiar religious boundary. The Samaritan’s help does not require the story to erase the communities’ real differences.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / turned-question
The question turns from “who counts?” to “who acted?”

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / turned-question
The opening asks which people count as neighbours. Jesus's closing question asks which character acted as a neighbour. The focus changes from classifying people to examining their actions.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / neighbor-doing
Neighborliness is shown in what the helper does

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / neighbor-doing
The final question directs attention to mercy in action. In this story, being a neighbor is not merely having the right group label.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / jewish-mercy
Care for the stranger is already in Jewish Scripture

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / jewish-mercy
Leviticus connects love of strangers with Israel’s memory of being foreigners. Jesus and the lawyer discuss neighbour-love within that Jewish tradition.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / road-panel
Mercy crosses the boundary from within a Jewish conversation

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / road-panel
The Samaritan helps across a religious boundary. Jewish Scripture already teaches care for strangers.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / opening-complaint
Pharisees and scribes complain: Jesus welcomes sinners to meals

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / opening-complaint
Luke introduces these stories with a complaint: “This man welcomes sinners, and eats with them.” The people complaining are Pharisees and scribes in this scene. They are not a stand-in for every Jewish person.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / younger-plan
Younger son plans to ask for a servant’s job

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / younger-plan
The younger son has spent his inheritance. He plans to return, admit his wrong, and ask his father: “Make me as one of your hired servants.” He imagines returning in a worker’s place.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / father-runs
Father runs to meet the returning younger son

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / father-runs
“But while he was still far off, his father saw him and was moved with compassion, and ran, fell on his neck, and kissed him.” The greeting comes before the son’s spoken confession. The father later calls for a celebration.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / father-outside
Father goes out to plead with the angry older son

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / father-outside
The older son hears about the feast, becomes angry, and refuses to enter. “Therefore his father came out and begged him.” The father does not stay inside waiting for him to become less angry.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / older-complaint
Older son: years of service, but no feast for me

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / older-complaint
The older son says, “these many years I have served you,” and complains that he was never given even a young goat for a celebration. He calls the returning son “this your son” rather than his brother.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / father-answer
Father to older son: all I have is yours; he is your brother

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / father-answer
The father replies that the older son is always with him and that all he has is his. He says they should celebrate because “this, your brother, was dead, and is alive again. He was lost, and is found.” The story ends here, without telling us whether the older son enters.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / father-both
The father goes out to both sons

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / father-both
The father runs toward the returning son and goes outside to the angry son. His care is directed toward both, not only the son who came home.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / not-hired
The welcome is not a negotiated job offer

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / not-hired
The younger son imagines returning as a hired servant, but the father welcomes him before such an arrangement is made. The order of the scene matters without making the son's change of heart irrelevant.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / service-relationship
The son counts service; the father names a relationship

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / service-relationship
The older son complains that his years of service have not earned a celebration. The father answers that he still belongs and that the returning son is his brother. The complaint is heard, not simply ignored.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / complaint-echo
The older brother echoes the complaint before the story

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / complaint-echo
The older brother objects to welcoming his brother home, while the earlier complainants object to Jesus welcoming people they call sinners. Both passages ask who should be welcomed.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / critics-invited
The welcome reaches toward the critics too

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / critics-invited
The father goes out to invite the angry older brother as well as welcoming the returning son. Read alongside the opening complaint about Jesus's meals, this may invite the critics to join the welcome.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / feast-not-wages
The feast is explained as reunion, not a wage calculation

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / feast-not-wages
The father celebrates someone lost being found. Neither the younger son's proposed job nor the older son's years of service explain the feast as a payment of wages.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / open-door-reading
The last invitation is still unanswered

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / open-door-reading
The father invites the older brother to the reunion, but Luke does not say whether he enters. The ending leaves his response open rather than reporting that everyone reconciled.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / door-panel
The expected insider may be the one still outside

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / door-panel
The Samaritan helps across a religious boundary, while the older brother stays outside his own family's feast. Being an expected insider does not necessarily mean acting as a neighbour or joining the welcome.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / mark-compassion
Mark: compassion leads to teaching; feeding follows

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / mark-compassion
Mark says that Jesus sees the crowd and is moved with compassion because they are “like sheep without a shepherd.” He begins to teach them. The feeding follows this scene.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / mark-feeding
Mark: five loaves, two fish, everyone filled, twelve baskets

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / mark-feeding
Mark reports that Jesus blesses and breaks five loaves and two fish, the disciples distribute them, and everyone eats and is filled. Twelve baskets of pieces remain; the account names five thousand men. It presents an extraordinary feeding, not simply an ordinary shared lunch.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / john-feeding
John: Passover is near; barley loaves feed the crowd

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / john-feeding
John places the feeding near Passover. A boy has five barley loaves and two fish. Jesus gives thanks; the people eat as much as they want, and twelve baskets remain. They call it a sign and want to make Jesus king, but he withdraws.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / passover-memory
Passover recalls Israel’s liberation from slavery in Egypt

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / passover-memory
Passover is a Jewish festival remembering the Exodus: Israel’s liberation from slavery in Egypt. John’s mention of the festival gives his feeding account a particular scriptural setting. Passover is still a living Jewish observance, not only a Christian symbol.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / manna-memory
Exodus: bread from heaven in the wilderness

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / manna-memory
In Exodus, the Israelites receive manna as food in the wilderness. The crowd in John's later story brings up this earlier account of bread from heaven.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / john-bread
John: “Our fathers ate the manna” ... “I am the bread of life”

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / john-bread
After the feeding, John records a discussion of manna. Jesus says it was not Moses who gave the bread from heaven, but his Father, and then says, “I am the bread of life.” John’s account develops a claim about who Jesus is.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / compassion-care
Compassion moves toward a person or a crowd

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / compassion-care
The Samaritan's compassion leads to practical care for a traveller. In Mark, Jesus's compassion leads to teaching and feeding the crowd. Both stories connect compassion with a response to people's needs.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / mark-reading
Mark moves from compassion to teaching and feeding

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / mark-reading
Mark’s narrative places the extraordinary feeding after compassion and teaching. Keeping that sequence visible makes the meal part of his portrayal of Jesus caring for the crowd.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / passover-meal
John places the feeding beside the memory of the Exodus

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / passover-meal
John places the feeding near Passover, connecting it with Israel's memory of liberation from slavery in Egypt.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / bread-comparison
John makes the earlier bread story a question about Jesus

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / bread-comparison
The crowd recalls the manna given in the wilderness. Jesus responds about his Father's gift and calls himself the bread of life. John's dialogue connects the earlier account of food with Jesus' claim about who he is.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / john-reading
John develops the meal through Passover and bread from heaven

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / john-reading
The festival setting and the later manna discussion connect the meal to Israel’s Scriptures. John develops an account of Jesus’ identity through those connections.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / bread-panel
The same feeding receives distinct emphases

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / bread-panel
Both Gospels report an extraordinary feeding. Mark places it after compassion and teaching, using the image of a shepherd. John connects it with Passover and the discussion of bread from heaven, developing his account of who Jesus is.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / luther-43
Luther, 1517: helping the poor is better than buying an indulgence

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / luther-43
In 1517, Luther says that giving to a poor person or lending to someone in need is better than buying an indulgence. An indulgence concerned penalties associated with sin, not permission to commit a new sin. His statement gives practical help real value.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / headline-draft
Draft headline: “Luther says good works do not matter”

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / headline-draft
The exhibition draft claims that Luther thinks good works do not matter. His own statement praises practical help for people in need. Check whether the headline fairly represents what he says.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / proof-paper
Blank proof paper

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / proof-paper
Blank paper for checking the passage before printing it for the exhibit.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / exhibit-folio
The four-panel exhibit

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / exhibit-folio
The four completed panels compare the roadside story, the family feast, the feeding accounts and Luther's statement about practical help.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / challenged-headline
This passage contradicts the sweeping headline

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / challenged-headline
Luther calls helping people in need a better deed. That contradicts the draft's blanket claim that good works do not matter, without settling all his views on faith and salvation.

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / tile/reader-caption / history-panel
A fairer headline: Luther commends practical help

## divinity-light-reading-scenes-v4 / reader-body / history-panel
